window.harnessChunks["rows-de92f51aea3be369.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The AI Scientist</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist/tree/1de1dbc1f4ee2c5f61e9c94348d55eb51d7fa2eb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1de1dbc1f4ee2c5f61e9c94348d55eb51d7fa2eb\">1de1dbc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement and execute research experiments and produce the resulting scientific artifacts within a maintained experiment template. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code/runtime errors, differing experimental ideas, metric outcomes, plotting/write-up needs and project-file state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete experiment/code edits and subsequent actions based on the idea, baseline state and observed execution/results. - Decision owner: the active model-driven Aider coding/experiment agent used by the first-party experiment loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: copied per-idea workspace, baseline results, performexperiments, Aider file editing, process execution, notes/logs and bounded experiment flow. - Closure path: idea/baseline/workspace -&gt; agent edits experiment implementation -&gt; code executes -&gt; result/error evidence returns -&gt; agent chooses further experiment edits or completion. - Boundary reachability: the loop is invoked by the documented launchscientist.py entrypoint for every novel idea in a standard paper-generation run. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Python launcher logic enforces sequencing, but the context-sensitive experiment/code choices are model-agent decisions. - Evidence: launchscientist.py, README.md, aiscientist/performexperiments.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple ideas may execute in parallel on different GPUs, but the workers are independent attempts; no concrete cross-S1 conflict/oscillation and attenuation relation is evidenced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: multiprocessing queue, GPU assignment and independent per-idea workspaces. - Closure path: scheduling distributes independent jobs; it does not return a coordination decision to change behavior because of inter-S1 interference. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: resource scheduling is deterministic runtime support and parallelism alone is not S2. - Evidence: launchscientist.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no agent-owned whole-system current-control function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failures/success at experiment/write-up/review stages change control flow, but stage ordering and stop/advance rules are fixed by the launcher. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous actor is shown holding a whole-run current view and discretionary authority over current shared resources, commitments, priorities or constraints. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: launcher stage sequence, exceptions/return values, GPU/process setup, skip/improvement flags and fixed success gates. - Closure path: deterministic success/failure and CLI configuration drive stage transitions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: local agents decide experiments/write-up content, while whole-run control remains scripted. - Evidence: launchscientist.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the produced research manuscript and, in the supported improvement mode, return findings into a separate corrective write-up path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported/unclear claims, presentation weaknesses, methodological weaknesses and other defects observable by a paper reviewer but not necessarily corrected during ordinary write-up. - Decisive decision or feedback right: produce a structured peer-review judgment/weakness set over the completed paper; those findings become explicit corrective input in improvement mode. - Decision owner: the first-party automated paper-review invocation, separate from the Aider writer/coder session being reviewed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PDF loading, review ensemble/reflections, persisted review.txt, performimprovement, PDF regeneration and second review. - Closure path: writer produces PDF -&gt; separate reviewer evaluates it -&gt; structured review/weaknesses -&gt; performimprovement(review, coder) returns findings to writer/coder -&gt; revised PDF -&gt; reviewer evaluates the revised artifact again. - Boundary reachability: --improvement is an explicit supported launcher mode using first-party review and improvement functions; it requires no external custom orchestration beyond normal model credentials. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer owns the semantic challenge judgment; the launcher only transfers review findings and enforces the second pass. - Claim being audited: that the research manuscript adequately presents/supports the generated scientific work at peer-review quality. - Ordinary reporting path: experiment notes/results -&gt; manuscript generation by the coding/writing agent. - Complementary access path: a separate review invocation reads the rendered paper and evaluates it with a review-specific prompt/ensemble rather than reusing the writer conversation. - Independence boundary: writer/coder and reviewer are separate model interactions/roles; review is generated after the artifact is materialized. - Who acts on findings: the Aider writer/coder through performimprovement, followed by manuscript regeneration. - Evidence: launchscientist.py, aiscientist/performreview.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: generate prospective research options, interrogate external literature for novelty and return novel ideas into current experimentation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: existing prior work may make a candidate idea non-novel; alternative ideas differ in feasibility/interestingness and should change the next experiment attempted. - Decisive decision or feedback right: propose/refine research ideas and, during novelty checking, choose literature queries and decide whether each idea is novel enough to enter the experiment set. - Decision owner: first-party idea-generation/novelty-checking model agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: idea reflection archive, Semantic Scholar/OpenAlex search, structured idea JSON, novelty flag and launcher filtering to novelideas. - Closure path: template/task context -&gt; agent proposes/refines idea -&gt; external literature searches return evidence -&gt; novelty judgment marks idea -&gt; only novel ideas are dispatched into executable experiments. - Boundary reachability: idea generation and novelty checking are invoked by the standard launcher unless explicitly skipped; their JSON/novelty outputs directly gate experiment admission. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: search APIs provide external observations, but query selection, idea refinement and novelty judgment are model-agent decisions. - External distinction: retrieved titles/abstracts/metadata from Semantic Scholar or OpenAlex. - Future / prospective distinction: whether a proposed idea is sufficiently novel/feasible/interesting to justify a future experiment. - Adaptation option generated: structured experiment idea with title, experiment outline and quality ratings. - Path back into current capability / S3: launcher filters ideas to novelideas and runs each accepted idea through doidea experiment/write-up execution. - Evidence: aiscientist/generateideas.py, launchscientist.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: experiment templates, prompt/task descriptions, model/engine flags and safety/container advice constrain operation, but no identity-level issue is routed to an ultimate authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: template prompt.json, CLI options, maintained template structure and static safety guidance. - Closure path: configuration/prompt/template policy directly constrains work; no identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research agents can select ideas/experiments but do not own authority to redefine the system&#39;s organizational identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: README.md, launchscientist.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/ai-scientist.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T23:48:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nsidn98/LLaMAR\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LLaMAR</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nsidn98/LLaMAR/tree/d2b74cac79a0f070d87a150712f8e2ae174920be\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d2b74cac79a0f070d87a150712f8e2ae174920be\">d2b74ca &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute the embodied work required to complete the shared task through robot-specific actions in the environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: partial observations, object locations/states, navigation/manipulation requirements, action failures and changing environment state during a long-horizon episode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose each robot&#39;s next substantive environment action from the current observations/state and interpret returned success/failure when selecting what happens next. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven LLaMAR action/controller path over the embodied robot streams. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AI2-THOR/SAR environment adapters, feasible-action mapping, per-agent observations/states/action histories, task/subtask state and deterministic execution plumbing. - Closure path: team task/current plan -&gt; model selects an action for each robot -&gt; environment executes each embodied action -&gt; per-agent observation/state/success/failure returns -&gt; later model call selects changed robot actions -&gt; episode progresses. - Boundary reachability: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py and SAR/baselines/llamar.py are shipped runnable first-party episode entrypoints and directly execute the credited action/environment feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: simulator code transports actions and state, while the substantive next-action choices are model-generated from the live episode context. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py; SAR/baselines/llamar.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: next-action authority is centralized in the model call rather than separately modelled inside each physical robot, but the operational behavior is still first-party agent-owned at the assessed team boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concrete interference among simultaneously operating embodied robots sharing the same physical workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: robots blocking/bumping one another, congestion around shared objects/receptacles, one robot&#39;s manipulation/navigation preventing another robot from completing its assigned action, and unnecessary simultaneous movement into the same constrained area. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose joint next-step actions such as waiting, moving aside, closing an obstruction or delaying one robot so another robot can proceed without cross-agent interference. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven joint action/controller path using whole-team state and failure context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared observations/state, previous action/failure fields, combined task/memory state, explicit anti-blocking prompt constraints and per-robot joint action output. - Closure path: multiple robot operations interact in one room -&gt; execution exposes cross-agent blocking/failure -&gt; the next model call reasons about the interference -&gt; assigns compensating actions across the involved robots -&gt; later robot behavior changes and the blocked operation can proceed. - Boundary reachability: the standard LLaMAR actor prompt is called on every episode step from the runnable baseline and directly receives/returns the team state and per-agent joint actions used by env.step(). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the anti-interference choice is contextual model reasoning over current cross-agent state; deterministic code only executes the selected joint actions. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic task allocation is not the basis for S2; the positive state rests on the separately evidenced physical interference problem and feedback relation. - Distinct S1 units: the concurrently acting embodied robot streams (Alice, Bob and additional configured robots), each with its own observation/state/action/success history and physical action effect. - Inter-S1 disturbance: one robot can physically block/bump another or occupy/open an object/path such that another robot&#39;s navigation/manipulation fails or must wait. - Attenuating coordination relation: the joint controller sees all robots&#39; state/history and is explicitly instructed to avoid blocking/bumping; failure reasoning can prescribe wait/move-aside/clear-path behavior across the involved robots. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the model&#39;s coordinated per-robot action dictionary is passed through actionchecker and then to env.step(), changing the next embodied action of each robot. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the decisive witness is regulation of a concrete interaction disturbance among simultaneous embodied operations, not the existence of multiple robots, shared memory or a task decomposition graph.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain the whole team&#39;s current task commitments and intervene in the active multi-robot plan as completion/failure/environment information changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing team progress, completed versus still-open commitments, newly observed environment information and failures requiring the active plan or current robot allocation/actions to change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the current shared open-subtask plan for the team and, through the whole-team action/controller stage, determine which operational action each robot should take now under that plan. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven planner and whole-team action/controller path. - Whole-system current view: the planner receives the task, current observation from every configured robot, and the shared open/completed subtask state after each verifier step; the action/controller additionally receives every robot&#39;s state, previous action/failure, shared subtasks and combined memory. - Current-control decision scope: preserve or revise the team&#39;s active unfinished commitments and assign the next concrete actions/subtasks across the current robot population, including interventions after failures and current spatial conflicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared open/completed-subtask state, verifier updates, whole-team observation aggregation, combined memory, planner prompt, action/controller prompt and repeated plan-update loop. - Closure path: robot operations execute -&gt; team observations/actions and completion state update -&gt; verifier updates completed commitments -&gt; planner forms a revised whole-team open plan -&gt; action/controller maps that current plan/state to robot-specific next actions -&gt; changed operations execute and report back. - Boundary reachability: the planner/action stages are invoked in every standard runnable LLaMAR episode; no external orchestrator is required to form the whole-team view or return interventions into robot operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the semantic choice of the current plan and per-robot intervention is model-driven; deterministic code stores plan/completion state and transports decisions into the environment. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py; SAR/baselines/llamar.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S3 credit is not based on the component name Planner; it rests on the whole-team current view plus returned decisions changing the active shared commitments and robot actions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: the verifier provides in-band completion reporting, but no materially independent complementary-audit function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect assumptions about whether a subtask has completed can be corrected before replanning, but the checked claim remains part of the ordinary task-progress loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent auditor with a distinct authority/evidence channel is established; the verifier prompt only classifies completed subtasks for the same episode. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3; the verifier uses the ordinary model service/configuration and shared episode evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verifier-specific prompt, all robot observations/states/previous actions, shared open/completed subtasks and combined memory. - Closure path: robot actions execute -&gt; ordinary episode observations feed verifier -&gt; completed-subtask list feeds planner -&gt; current plan changes; this is ordinary progress reporting/monitoring without an evidenced independent audit boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a semantic verifier exists, but verifier naming and a separate prompt-stage do not establish complementary independence. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the verifier does influence subsequent planning; the missing requirement is organizational/complementary independence, not lack of feedback.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party prospective capability-adaptation loop is established beyond current-episode planning/replanning. - Disturbance / variety regulated: LLaMAR adapts its current task plan to new observations and failures, but does not evidence an actor that senses an external future environment, generates capability-change options and installs a change for later operational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for prospective adaptation of the installed harness/robot organization across episodes. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current observation updates, planner replanning, memory and failure reasoning. - Closure path: environment change/failure -&gt; current episode replanning -&gt; changed immediate robot actions; no future-oriented capability option is implemented into later installed capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current replanning is S1/S3 regulation, not evidence of S4 merely because the plan changes. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; README.md. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: long-horizon planning and replanning are substantial capabilities but remain inside the present episode boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity or ultimate-policy authority is established at the multi-robot episode boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task text, model/configuration parameters, prompt rules and simulator limits constrain behavior but do not constitute identity-level adjudication. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for defining or reconciling the team&#39;s ultimate identity/purpose/policy under competing S3/S4 demands. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user-selected task/scene/configuration, static prompts and runtime constraints. - Closure path: external task/configuration enters directly as operating constraints; no identity-level issue -&gt; legitimate ultimate authority -&gt; policy decision -&gt; returned governance loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the models control current operational/coordination/management choices but do not choose the organization&#39;s ultimate identity or policy. - Evidence: README.md; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: static system prompts describing robot roles/constraints are not S5 by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a6735e44e0989bceeb433c4133d76671108cc563/assessments/llamar.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a6735e44e0989bceeb433c4133d76671108cc563\">a6735e4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T16:28:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DemonDamon/AgenticX\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgenticX</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DemonDamon/AgenticX/tree/582c9d00bfb23efe6f7e588c3a5edfce570729bf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 582c9d00bfb23efe6f7e588c3a5edfce570729bf\">582c9d0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended user tasks by choosing tools, using execution results, and modifying task artifacts through root/child provider-backed agent sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new task details, tool errors, repository content, provider responses and subtask outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next model tool call, interpret feedback and continue/revise an execution plan. - Decision owner: configured model-backed AgentRuntime/ReAct root and spawned agents; neither the SDK Agent data model nor the event queue owns model reasoning. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider resolver, agent/tool loop, Studio session persistence, subagent context/tool permissions and callbacks. - Closure path: model chooses a tool, runtime executes it, the result enters the same agent&#39;s context, and later decisions change task output. - Boundary reachability: this path is shipped both through the agx/Studio local backend and embedded SDK; Near&#39;s default app starts this local runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent model makes contextual tool/continuation decisions; host code supplies guardrails and transport. - Evidence: AgentRuntime, ReActAgent, Studio server Meta-Agent setup. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: successful live provider/model execution and claimed production quality were not measured.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidence-backed distinct relation that autonomously attenuates an identified interference among simultaneously acting S1 units, beyond task partition and bounded queueing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential overlap of parallel workers, resource contention and conflicting changes would require interference-specific handling. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party agent-owned cross-worker conflict detection, reconciliation or isolation decision shown as an operationally closed coordination loop for the declared Studio team. - Decision owner: none established at S2; the model planner assigns tasks and host TaskLock transports actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Team task decomposition, worker assignments/dependencies, TaskLock action queue, group routing and shared context. - Closure path: delegations can complete and summaries return, but that is ordinary task routing; no distinct detected inter-S1 collision and corrective relation is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming a CoordinatorAgent or serializing actions does not establish function-specific S2 conflict attenuation. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent model-backed child agents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: same-file edits or contested resources are plausible, but the inspected supported team path does not independently establish a response to an observed conflict. - Attenuating coordination relation: no qualifying inter-S1 relation established; task lists and a queue prevent generic scheduling races, not semantically competing work ownership. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: worker results and messages return to the manager/leader, but without a verified conflict-specific control decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: S2-specific ownership is not credited for generic dispatch or action queue operations. - Evidence: Team mode wiring and TaskLock, TaskLock, WorkforcePattern. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: developers may compose custom S2 adapters with the SDK; that is not automatically the shipped Studio default.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current root-session team by observing children and deciding when to spawn, communicate, retry or cancel during live task delivery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: active child runs may stall/fail, saturate concurrency or CPU/memory, need new work or require intervention to meet the immediate user task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and invoke status query, spawn/cancel/retry/message subagent tools in light of reported current child state and goals. - Decision owner: configured root model-backed Meta-Agent within the supported Studio session, not the AgentTeamManager or the deterministic ResourceMonitor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentTeamManager actual live status registry, timeouts, caps and resource admission checks; root Meta-Agent tool dispatcher; Studio event stream. - Closure path: root sees team status/tool results, chooses a current-team intervention, AgentTeamManager changes a child run/permission to launch, and updated state returns to the root&#39;s next model turn. - Boundary reachability: Studio builds the root Meta-Agent with visible team-management tools, and the attached AgentTeamManager executes them on real session children in the shipped local backend. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current worker-team decisions are controlled by the root model, while deterministic caps merely reject inadmissible actions. - Whole-system current view: querysubagentstatus and team status provide running/completed/failed child state and resource-monitor context for the session team. - Current-control decision scope: the current portfolio of active/retryable child agents in one root session, including admission and cessation; not Enterprise gateway quotas or an unrelated Studio user. - Evidence: team lifecycle/status/cancel, meta tool handler, resource monitor. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the model might choose not to use these tools; the claim is an available integrated operational loop, not a guaranteed policy of continuous proactive optimization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently controlled complementary reality-audit path with corrective control established for the current Studio session team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an executor can mistakenly report complete work while the real environment differs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent auditor&#39;s direct-evidence veto/intervention against the team report established in the selected operating mode. - Decision owner: none established; SDK evaluation/LLM judge and Enterprise audit logging are separate or routine surfaces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: traces, tool results, observability and optional evaluation framework. - Closure path: tests/evaluations can be explicitly launched, but a first-party independently owned standing complementary team audit with returned corrective control is not demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: evaluation APIs, group worker results and log chains cannot substitute for a separate auditor owner. - Evidence: evaluation/observability overview, team results, Enterprise gateway separation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: externally composed independent reviewers are possible through the SDK, but their independence cannot be credited to the shipped session team.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: convert observations of changing external tool/provider/task conditions and user corrections into future reusable skill capability for subsequent sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated external tool failures, newly learned environment conventions and multi-step strategies that existing installed skills do not yet encode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the background model-backed review agent decides whether and how to create/patch a reusable skill; under the normal supervised mode the Desktop operator separately decides whether to approve the proposed future capability. - Decision owner: opt-in skill-review agent in the direct-authorized mode (A), and legitimate Desktop-user approver for a separately supported queued-proposal mode (P). These modes are not simultaneous autonomous governance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registered SessionReviewHook, actual tool observation records, signal/complexity gate, configured review model, skillmanage, skill policy scan, pending proposals and desktop approval endpoint. - Closure path: a completed tool-using session triggers a review of observed attempts/errors/user corrections; the model proposes a skill; in explicitly authorized direct mode it writes the skill for later sessions, while in default queue mode the Desktop user approves the same proposed document and installs it. Later model runs can discover/use the changed skill collection. - External distinction: observations concern user corrections and tool/provider outcomes from the task environment, not just the host runtime&#39;s own counters; the model interprets their reusable implications. - Future / prospective distinction: a post-session reviewer encodes a method for later task contexts rather than repairing only the completed current task, and the installed skill survives that session boundary. - Adaptation option generated: choose a new action-oriented skill or a patch to an existing skill, including the specific steps and guardrails for future comparable disturbances. - Path back into current capability / S3: after authorized direct installation or Desktop-user approval, the skill is added to the local installed registry so a later Meta-Agent/runtime session can discover and use the revised capability under its current task controls. - Boundary reachability: AgentRuntime registers SessionReviewHook; learning.reviewenabled is an active shipped configuration option, agentwritesrequireapproval distinguishes queue versus direct install, and Studio/Near expose proposal inspection/approve/reject routes. Both paths require configured model/provider access; neither is default-on just because observation is enabled. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer model selects substantive future reusable methods, not just a deterministic score; the operator retains the separate approval right in the supervised mode.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no closed ultimate-purpose or identity-policy governance path for the local Studio agent-team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts about the team&#39;s permanent charter, legitimacy or non-negotiable identity beyond ordinary tasks/permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no runtime identity-level final authority and returned policy choice established in the selected local session hierarchy. - Decision owner: none established for this function; product maintainers, Enterprise administrators and local user are adjacent authorities for different matters. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: operator permissions, skill-approval gate, safety policies, user prompts, Enterprise Gateway policies and contributor governance. - Closure path: users permit/deny operations, administrators configure Gateway routing, and skill approvals modify future capabilities; these are operational/safety/adaptation choices rather than a reconstructed identity-level decision loop in local Studio. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a Meta-Agent CEO label does not create sovereign identity control, and separate enterprise governance does not transfer to deployed Studio S5. - Evidence: runtime/Enterprise boundaries, Studio tool gates, Meta-Agent identity prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no finding about how the open-source project or enterprise tenants should normatively govern themselves is made.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d83ab15f2643bc53884a269c9555beaea45091fb/assessments/ag/agenticx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d83ab15f2643bc53884a269c9555beaea45091fb\">d83ab15 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T17:45:27Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pipecat</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat/tree/dbdf21a017f86624fb7768e35730417169524e0d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dbdf21a017f86624fb7768e35730417169524e0d\">dbdf21a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform an environment-facing conversational/agent task by selecting model responses and model-callable tools from live input/context and incorporating tool/service outcomes into subsequent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user utterances, conversation state, tool needs/results, external-service responses, media events and task-specific runtime outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive next response/tool action in the shipped model/tool loop. - Decision owner: the configured LLM actor invoked through Pipecat&#39;s first-party LLM service/pipeline surfaces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: context aggregators; tool schemas/callbacks; transports; pipeline frames; jobs; timeouts; service adapters; tracing/metrics. - Closure path: user/environment input enters pipeline → Pipecat constructs model context/tools → model chooses response/tool action → runtime executes/delivers it → observations/results are returned into later context and operation. - Boundary reachability: this is Pipecat&#39;s ordinary supported runtime, not a repository-development-only example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model decision actor leaves transport/execution machinery but removes the open-ended choice of conversational/task action. - Evidence: README.md; llmworker.py; openclaw-agent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider implementation is external, but Pipecat deliberately places the model actor inside its supported operational feedback loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concurrent/interleaved output interference between distinct autonomous LLM workers during a live handoff and ensure that only the selected worker owns the next conversational operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the target worker can begin producing while the source worker still has output in flight, causing both workers&#39; outputs to arrive interleaved; overlapping activation would make ownership of the user-facing turn ambiguous. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide when to transfer conversational ownership from the current autonomous worker to another worker in response to the live conversation. - Decision owner: the currently active LLM worker in the first-party local-handoff mode, through the model-callable transfertoagent tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: activateworker; source-pipeline drain; mutually exclusive activation/deactivation; shared worker registry/bus; tool result that suppresses another current LLM run during transfer. - Closure path: current LLM observes conversation → model selects transfertoagent → source worker drains outstanding output → runtime deactivates source and activates target without overlap → subsequent user-facing operation is produced by the selected target worker. - Boundary reachability: the behavior is implemented by first-party worker APIs and exercised by a runnable first-party multi-worker example whose documentation explicitly states that the LLM decides when to transfer. - Distinct S1 units: the active greeter/customer-service LLMWorker and the target support LLMWorker are separate model-driven operational workers sharing the conversation transport through the worker runtime. - Inter-S1 disturbance: without handoff drainage/exclusivity, the target worker can begin producing while the source worker still has output in flight, and both outputs can arrive interleaved. - Attenuating coordination relation: activateworker drains the source pipeline, deactivates it, then activates the selected target so the two workers do not simultaneously own user-facing output. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: after the model-selected handoff, the target worker becomes active and receives/acts on subsequent conversation turns while the source is inactive. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism exists to attenuate a documented interference between two autonomous operational workers, and its result changes their subsequent operational participation; credit does not rely on WorkerBus messaging or delegation alone. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces safe switching, but the semantic choice of when/where to transfer is made by the active model from live conversational evidence. Removing that model decision leaves a callable handoff primitive, not the same coordination judgment. - Evidence: local-handoff-two-agents.py; worker.py; baseworker.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic WorkerBus messaging/job routing is not used as S2 evidence; credit depends on the concrete interleaving disturbance and the model-selected handoff closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current subordinate agent commitment while preserving a responsive voice/conversation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: long-running delegated work can be idle, active, obsolete after a user correction, redirected, cancelled, failed or completed while the conversational front end continues receiving new input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: from live subordinate-job state, choose whether to dispatch/update work, inspect current status or preemptively stop the active subordinate commitment. - Decision owner: the voice LLM in the first-party OpenClaw-agent composition; its context exposes sendtoagent, stopagent and agentstatus as model-callable tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ActiveJob; bus job requests/updates/responses; job-group cancellation; WorkerRunner; OpenClaw worker; developer messages that return job outcomes to the voice model. - Closure path: voice LLM receives current user input plus returned job state/outcome → model chooses direct response or a current-control tool → Pipecat dispatches/updates, reports or cancels the subordinate job → bus update/response changes ActiveJob and is returned to the voice loop → later current-control decisions use the changed state. - Boundary reachability: the complete path is a runnable first-party multi-worker example built on public first-party worker/job APIs. - Whole-system current view: at the selected voice-plus-agent recursion, the voice regulator tracks the only subordinate operational commitment (jobid, request, start time and later response/update state) while continuing its own user-facing work. - Current-control decision scope: it can create/change the live delegated commitment and preempt it; this is stronger than read-only status or runtime lifecycle cleanup. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the LLM itself selects the exposed dispatch/status/stop tools from current conversational evidence. Runtime code transports/enforces those choices; it does not make an equivalent semantic judgment when the model is removed. - Evidence: openclaw-agent/README.md; openclaw-agent.py; runner.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the OpenClaw backend decides whether a forwarded update starts a new internal turn or steers an existing one. S3 credit is not assigned to that backend decision; it is assigned to Pipecat&#39;s model-facing authority to observe, issue/update and cancel the current subordinate commitment at this recursion. Generic WorkerRunner end/cancel behavior alone would be constructor lifecycle support, not agent-owned S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit loop was established that independently inspects current operational claims and returns findings into the live operation/current-control loop for corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Pipecat can evaluate bot conversations and report assertion/judge failures, but those evaluation outcomes are separate test/eval results rather than a shipped production correction loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3 at the assessed runtime boundary. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scripted and simulated eval drivers; optional EvalJudge; expectation matching; pass/fail result objects; traces; ordinary worker job responses/errors. - Closure path: no S3-specific corrective closure established. Eval failures can stop or continue an evaluation scenario and are reported to the evaluator/caller, but the reviewed runtime does not feed a distinct auditor finding back into the audited bot&#39;s current work to require revision/retry. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a separate judge exists, but independence alone is insufficient; the missing production feedback closure prevents classification as A or C S3. - Evidence: scriptdriver.py; simulation.py; openclaw-agent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream applications can consume eval failures and implement corrective loops; caller composition is not credited as first-party Pipecat closure without shipped wiring.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop was established that converts environmental distinctions into persistent changes of Pipecat operating capability/policy used by later work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: persistent conversation memory and dynamic current-session flow/context exist, but no reviewed mechanism closes prospective capability adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mem0 message storage/search; LLM conversation context; flow transitions and role messages; runtime LLM settings updates; session continuation. - Closure path: no S4-specific closure established. Mem0 stores user/assistant messages and retrieves relevant memories into later context; it does not itself decide or persist a changed skill/tool/policy/capability in response to external/future distinctions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory continuity and current-flow reconfiguration can affect future responses, but the reviewed first-party code does not contain the required sensing → prospective adaptation judgment → persistent capability change → reuse loop. - Evidence: memory.py; flows/manager.py; llmcontext.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream agent can use Pipecat tools to build its own learning/adaptation system; that is outside this standalone runtime assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy loop with legitimate ultimate authority and returned closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: bots can have durable system instructions, role messages and application-authored rules, but these are configured operating instructions rather than an S5 adjudication loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an S5 witness would require an unresolved identity/ultimate-policy issue to reach a legitimate parent/ultimate authority and the resulting policy decision to return into later operation; no such first-party path was established. - Decision owner: not established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured systeminstruction; Flow rolemessage; tool schemas; application code; provider/session settings. - Closure path: no S5-specific escalation-and-return path established. Pipecat can apply or update instructions supplied by the application, but that is instruction transport/current configuration rather than ownership of identity or ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither static developer-authored identity nor runtime system-instruction mutation by framework/application code establishes a legitimate ultimate-policy decision loop under Profile 0.2.4. - Evidence: flows/manager.py; llmservice.py; uiworker.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream product owners may govern bot identity out of band; this assessment does not convert arbitrary application configuration into Pipecat S5 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2f9af52d3fefcbb27eb35f8f736f06a8ef93e432/assessments/pipecat.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2f9af52d3fefcbb27eb35f8f736f06a8ef93e432\">2f9af52 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:24:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/autogen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft AutoGen AgentChat</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/tree/027ecf0a379bcc1d09956d46d12d44a3ad9cee14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 027ecf0a379bcc1d09956d46d12d44a3ad9cee14\">027ecf0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: stateful agents choose tools/actions and incorporate returned results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: team selection plus Magentic-One&#39;s explicit loop/stall detection and controlled transfer of the next action right suppress uncontrolled repetition/interference among multiple S1 participants. This is more than mere delegation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: MagenticOneOrchestrator owns a whole-team current facts/plan/progress ledger and has authority to assess progress, choose commitments, reset participants and regenerate the plan when the team stalls. Code-execution approval constrains an operational executor action rather than independently owning this whole-team current-control loop, so no separate (P) mode is published.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: the progress ledger is part of the same operational S3 regulator that directs the team. It is not a distinct complementary audit channel with separate reviewing independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: replanning after current-task stalls is inside-now adaptation. No distinct external/prospective intelligence function shaping future organizational posture is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: participant set, instructions, termination rules and ultimate authority remain application/parent-owned.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9/assessments/autogen-agentchat.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9\">9ee1ea7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Technion-Kishony-lab/data-to-paper\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">data-to-paper</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Technion-Kishony-lab/data-to-paper/tree/81df14c4b9600466e645c3b2b336cc54daa3df3a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 81df14c4b9600466e645c3b2b336cc54daa3df3a\">81df14c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform the environment-facing scientific work needed to transform project data and research state into executable analyses and research artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous datasets, generated-code errors, unexpected outputs/files, statistical-analysis requirements, intermediate product state and manuscript construction requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise analysis/code/content actions in response to current project products, execution results and validation/reviewer feedback. - Decision owner: the active first-party performer/code-writing agents in the hypothesis-testing workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stage runner, CodeRunner, debugger conversers, code extract/check logic, output requirements, product store, data chaining and configured iteration bounds. - Closure path: current scientific products/data -&gt; agent proposes code/content action -&gt; first-party runner executes/materializes it -&gt; output/error/product state returns -&gt; agent revises or advances. - Boundary reachability: these paths are the shipped hypothesis-testing research workflow invoked by the installable application/CLI, not benchmark-only examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime and guardrails execute/check code, but the context-sensitive analysis/content choice and revision remain model-agent decisions. - Evidence: README.md, stepsrunner.py, requestcode.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the workflow contains multiple performer/reviewer roles and research stages, but no concrete interference, conflict or oscillation among distinct S1 units is identified and attenuated by a dedicated coordination relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed stage transitions, product passing, paired conversations, deterministic sequencing and rewind/retry utilities. - Closure path: these mechanisms sequence work and transmit review feedback; they do not close a cross-S1 interference-regulation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: interacting agents and state transfer are real, but the reviewed paths are production/review dependencies rather than S2-specific conflict attenuation. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, dualconverser.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no autonomous whole-system inside-and-now control function is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stage completion, retries, rewinds, project parameters and review rounds constrain current work, but no first-party autonomous actor is shown holding a whole-project current view plus broad authority over shared resources/commitments/priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: stage flow and retry bounds are primarily encoded in deterministic runner logic and project parameters; local reviewers control product acceptance rather than organization-wide current control. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DataStepRunner/HypothesisTestingStepsRunner, stage enums, product readiness, retry/rewind helpers, project parameters and UI actions. - Closure path: configured stage/retry conditions alter control flow mechanically; no discretionary whole-system S3 owner is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents make local scientific/product decisions, while the broader workflow sequence is framework-defined. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, README.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: provide a separate challenge channel that reviews scientific/code/writing products and returns concrete corrective feedback before those products are accepted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible-but-wrong analysis outputs, code/result problems, weak hypotheses/plans, writing defects and other product claims that ordinary performer generation may miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently approve a product or issue constructive concerns that force another performer revision cycle. - Decision owner: the separate reviewer LLM conversation in ReviewDialogDualConverserGPT and review-specific subclasses; in code paths, review agents inspect executed outputs/code products separately from the generating/debugging conversation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate ConversationManager, review termination phrase, max review rounds, product extraction/checks, code-review prompts and rewind utilities. - Closure path: performer produces valid candidate -&gt; reviewer receives the candidate/background -&gt; reviewer approves or supplies concerns -&gt; concerns are appended to the performer conversation with explicit correction request -&gt; performer rewrites/revises -&gt; review repeats. - Boundary reachability: the review dialog base class is reused by goal, planning and manuscript-writing review steps in the shipped hypothesis-testing workflow; code review is also embedded in the standard code-product path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer is a separate agent conversation and owns the semantic approve/challenge judgment; deterministic loop code only routes/enforces that judgment. - Claim being audited: that a current scientific product/code result is adequate/correct enough to become accepted workflow state. - Ordinary reporting path: performer response plus normal generated/executed product state. - Complementary access path: separate reviewer conversation receives the product and relevant background/output context; code review can inspect created output contents. - Independence boundary: reviewer has its own conversation manager/role and does not share the performer&#39;s assistant role; review feedback is generated independently and then returned. - Who acts on findings: the original performer/code-writing agent revises the product in the next cycle. - Evidence: dualconverser.py, requestcode.py, stepsrunner.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: use external literature/novelty evidence to develop or revise future research direction and return the selected direction into the current experimental plan, with both autonomous and Copilot parent-governed modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: existing literature may make a candidate goal non-novel, external findings may change relevant hypotheses, and a human scientist may judge the proposed future direction inadequate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in Autopilot, research/novelty agents judge literature-derived novelty and generate/refine the goal/hypothesis; in Copilot, human review can replace/supplement reviewer feedback and require revised research products. - Decision owner: autonomous research/reviewer agents in base mode; human scientist in the supported Copilot parent mode for the reviewed future-direction decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: literature-search agents, NoveltyAssessmentReview, ReGoalReviewGPT, stage return to GOAL, human-review application interactions, product storage and plan handoff. - Closure path: external literature -&gt; novelty/goal review -&gt; autonomous or parent judgment -&gt; if inadequate, workflow returns to goal generation/refinement -&gt; resulting goal/hypothesis/testing plan feeds data analysis and later research stages. - Boundary reachability: literature/novelty/goal refinement is part of the shipped hypothesis-testing runner, and README plus HumanReviewType/review interaction code expose Copilot human feedback through the same first-party workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base mode closes without human direction through model-agent novelty/refinement decisions; the separately supported Copilot mode intentionally transfers the relevant review/judgment right to the human and returns that decision into workflow state. - External distinction: retrieved scientific literature and most-similar-paper evidence. - Future / prospective distinction: whether the current research goal/hypothesis is novel/appropriate enough to warrant future experimentation. - Adaptation option generated: revised research goal/hypothesis and downstream hypothesis-testing plan. - Path back into current capability / S3: failed novelty sets regoal and returns to ScientificStage.GOAL; accepted/revised goal then feeds planning, code analysis and manuscript stages. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, dualconverser.py, README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project parameters, research goals, human feedback and methodological guardrails constrain work, but no identity-level dispute is routed to an ultimate authority and returned as governing organizational policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: startup/project parameters, user research goal, coding guardrails, reviewer prompts and human-review UI. - Closure path: these controls constrain local research behavior directly; no identity/ultimate-policy issue -&gt; authoritative decision -&gt; returned policy loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither autonomous agents nor the Copilot human are shown resolving an identity-level policy issue at this recursion. - Evidence: README.md, stepsrunner.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/data-to-paper.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T19:11:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhayujie/CowAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CowAgent</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhayujie/CowAgent/tree/7c55a61e1bccb82c99d97a4a436f598c079f7c9d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7c55a61e1bccb82c99d97a4a436f598c079f7c9d\">7c55a61 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously transform user/scheduled/delegated objectives into environment-facing tool/skill work and returned outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user objectives, model uncertainty, tool results/errors, files/shell/browser/service state, channel messages, and follow-up observations encountered during a trajectory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next reasoning/tool/skill action, incorporate returned observations, delegate bounded work when useful, or stop with a final response. - Decision owner: the running CowAgent Agent/model actor inside the first-party AgentStreamExecutor loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ChatService, AgentBridge, provider adapters, prompt/workspace construction, tool registry, permissions, session persistence, scheduler integration and context controls. - Closure path: each tool result, delegated result or new model observation returns into the first-party executor context and can change the next autonomous action until completion. - Boundary reachability: the credited executor/bridge/tool path is the normal shipped Web/channel runtime and is instantiated directly by standard CowAgent operation at the pinned revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code supplies transport, limits and execution, while the model actor owns the discretionary next-action choice on each loop iteration. - Evidence: pinned agent/chat/service.py and bridge/agentbridge.py; README.md corroborates the shipped planning/tool execution boundary. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/provider/tool availability, permissions and iteration limits constrain the Agent&#39;s operational discretion but do not replace its next-action judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate a concrete interference between sibling operational Agents when concurrent delegations target the same persistent relay conversation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent handoffs to one source→target relay session would otherwise interleave turns in the target Agent&#39;s transcript and corrupt the shared conversational state used by later delegated work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: serialize admission to that target relay session so exactly one delegated turn occupies it at a time; timeout rather than permit an overlapping handoff when the lock cannot be acquired within policy. - Decision owner: constructor-owned at the assessed boundary. The first-party S2-specific serialization primitive is complete and operational, but the conflict policy itself is deterministic/developer-selected rather than autonomously chosen by an Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-relay relaylock, stable relay-session derivation, delegation roster/ACL, depth/cycle checks, timeout and synchronous result return. - Closure path: a source Agent&#39;s delegation blocks on the relay lock; after the prior target turn finishes and releases the lock, the next delegated target run executes against a non-interleaved transcript and its result returns to the waiting source Agent, changing that source&#39;s subsequent S1 behavior. - Boundary reachability: agentdelegate is a shipped team tool attached to normal team turns through AgentBridge; no downstream coordinator or test-only actor is required for the serialization path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents autonomously decide to delegate and select the teammate/task, but once two handoffs conflict the coordination choice is a fixed first-party lock policy. CowAgent therefore exposes a real S2-specific construction path without an autonomous owner of the conflict-regulation decision. - Evidence: pinned agent/tools/agentdelegate/agentdelegate.py and team-session support in bridge/agentbridge.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: other team features such as speaker selection, shared transcripts and ordinary handoff are not separately credited as S2 merely because they connect Agents. - Distinct S1 units: independently configured live CowAgent Agents with separate profiles/workspaces and first-party execution loops participating in one team conversation. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping delegated turns to the same target relay session can interleave in the target transcript. - Attenuating coordination relation: the first-party per-relay lock queues competing handoffs and allows only one target turn at a time. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the coordinated target result is returned synchronously to the delegating source Agent&#39;s tool loop; timeout/failure likewise returns a result that changes the source Agent&#39;s next decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the lock exists specifically because concurrent sibling handoffs would interfere by interleaving one operational transcript, and it changes admission/timing to suppress that identified disturbance before results feed back to the source Agent.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate the currently configured CowAgent installation as a whole by changing which operational Agents are available, which models/capabilities they use, and which live channel instances/teams route work to them. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current mismatch between installed operational capacity and the operator&#39;s desired roster, agent availability, model/capability allocation, shared/own knowledge mode, default ownership and live channel/team bindings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create/update/archive/delete an Agent, make an Agent default, enable/disable it, alter its model/skills/knowledge allocation, or rebind a running channel/team to a different Agent roster. - Decision owner: the authenticated self-hosted operator is the legitimate parent actor in this supported mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentAdminService, roster revision checks, Web authentication, registry/router rebuilding, scheduler reconciliation, targeted live-runtime eviction and hot channel rebinding. - Closure path: the operator submits a first-party Web/API decision; CowAgent persists it, updates the live registry/router or channel binding, starts/stops the affected schedulers and evicts changed Agent instances so subsequent current work is executed under the returned control decision. - Boundary reachability: the authenticated Web console and its /api/agents administration paths are a standard shipped self-hosted operating surface documented as the unified configuration hub, not a development-only control plane. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no standard first-party autonomous Agent is given installation-wide roster/model/capability/channel authority. The decisive current-control choice comes from the authenticated operator; runtime code only validates, persists and enforces it. - Evidence: pinned agent/admin.py, channel/web/api/agents.py, and bridge/agentbridge.py scheduler/runtime reconciliation. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this claim is limited to installation-level current capability/resource control. Per-task steering/cancel and permission enforcement alone would not establish S3, and the assessment does not treat every settings edit as an S3 decision. - Whole-system current view: AgentAdminService.snapshot() exposes the installation&#39;s full current Agent roster, default Agent and channel instances; the same authenticated surface operates over that roster rather than one worker-local state. - Current-control decision scope: availability of S1 units, current model/capability allocation, default Agent, channel/team routing and scheduler participation for the running installation.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary and sufficiently independent audit function was established at the declared installation boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: none established as an S3 witness; ordinary tool feedback, run/event recording and Self-Evolution review do not provide a materially independent route to operational reality for checking production claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S3-specific independent audit judgment/feedback right is supplied. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution/tool events, conversation/run persistence, evolution records/backups, logs and operator inspection surfaces. - Closure path: no independent audit finding→corrective-current-control loop was established; recorded events and evolution changes serve their primary execution/adaptation purposes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: there is no qualifying audit function whose decisive judgment could be assigned to an autonomous Agent. - Evidence: pinned chat/executor event path, conversation storage, Web/admin surfaces, and agent/evolution/ review path. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream deployment could add an independent verifier or external monitor, but that composed system would be outside this frozen first-party boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert externally observed conversation patterns and capability failures into prospective adaptations of reusable future capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user preferences and recurring work patterns, skill defects discovered in real use, reusable workflows not yet encoded as capabilities, and unfinished work whose recurrence or omission would reduce future fit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after an idle conversation, decide autonomously whether there is a clear durable signal and whether to patch an existing skill, create a new reusable skill, consolidate knowledge/memory, or take no adaptation action. - Decision owner: the isolated first-party Self-Evolution review Agent/model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: idle trigger thresholds, restricted evolution toolset, guarded workspace paths, pre-change backup, snapshot diff/change detection, transactional rollback and evolution records. - Closure path: the review Agent senses conversation-derived external signals, selects and writes an adaptation, the executor commits only real guarded changes, and later normal Agent runs load/use the revised skill/knowledge/persona state; if no clear signal exists it explicitly leaves capability unchanged. - Boundary reachability: Self-Evolution is started by normal AgentBridge initialization, its configuration class defaults enabled, and the standard pinned config-template.json ships selfevolutionenabled: true; the decision actor is therefore reachable in the supported distribution without borrowing a dogfood or contributor agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: trigger timing, backups and write guards are deterministic support, but the model review Agent owns the discretionary judgment of whether a past external interaction predicts future reusable need and which permitted adaptation to make. - Evidence: pinned agent/evolution/config.py, trigger.py, executor.py, prompts.py, bridge/agentbridge.py, config-template.json and docs/memory/self-evolution.mdx. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: simple memory consolidation alone is not the credited S4 witness. The positive mapping relies on the separately evidenced prospective skill-adaptation path: detecting a workflow likely to be wanted again or a skill defect exposed in use, autonomously generating an adaptation, and returning that change into later capability. - External distinction: the review consumes user conversation and actual skill/task experience from the environment, including repeated preferences, failures and emergent workflows. - Future / prospective distinction: the evolution prompt explicitly asks whether a workflow is reusable and likely to be wanted again, and documentation defines the goal as creating/fixing skills so they can be reused next time and future conversations improve. - Adaptation option generated: patch a defective skill, encode an emergent workflow as a new reusable skill, consolidate durable knowledge/memory, complete suitable unfinished work, or deliberately make no change. - Path back into current capability / S3: committed workspace/skill/knowledge changes are the same first-party artifacts read by subsequent normal Agent operation; operator rollback remains a separate parent intervention path rather than the owner of the autonomous adaptation judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: govern the Agent&#39;s durable identity/personality and highest-level workspace behavioral principles through first-party identity files that are loaded into subsequent system prompts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: drift between the Agent&#39;s durable identity/behavioral principles and explicit repeated signals about what the assistant should be, plus explicit operator decisions to redefine those identity/policy artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in the autonomous mode, decide whether an explicit repeated identity/personality/style signal is strong enough to justify a bounded AGENT.md identity revision; in the parent mode, authoritatively edit AGENT.md/RULE.md through the authenticated core-file administration surface. - Decision owner: base mode — the isolated Self-Evolution review Agent for the deliberately narrow autonomous identity-evolution decision; parent mode — the authenticated self-hosted operator as ultimate authority over the installation&#39;s core identity/policy files. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evolution signal criteria and guarded writes/backups; AgentAdminService core-file revision checks and atomic writes; authenticated Web endpoint; prompt/workspace loader; targeted runtime eviction after operator edits. - Closure path: autonomous mode: repeated identity-level external signal → review-Agent identity judgment → guarded AGENT.md edit → subsequent runtime loads the changed persona. Parent mode: authenticated operator reads/edits a core identity/policy file → atomic persistence and cached-Agent eviction → subsequent Agent instance reloads that file into its system prompt. - Boundary reachability: both modes are shipped supported runtime paths: default-enabled Self-Evolution may edit AGENT.md under its standard guard, while the authenticated Web Agents API exposes core-file read/write and immediately evicts the changed runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base identity change is not a static prompt or automatic parser; the review Agent must judge whether a rare explicit repeated signal warrants changing its own identity artifact. The separate operator mode retains parent authority to set or override the same identity/policy surface, hence A(P) rather than plain A. - Evidence: pinned agent/evolution/prompts.py, executor.py, agent/prompt/workspace.py, agent/prompt/builder.py, agent/admin.py and channel/web/api/agents.py. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ordinary permission settings, tool approvals, memory/preferences and per-task steering are not credited as S5. The autonomous claim is narrowly limited to the explicit first-party AGENT.md identity-evolution path; operator edits establish the separate parent mode. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: AGENT.md is explicitly defined by the shipped templates as the Agent&#39;s identity/personality and is loaded with RULE.md into the system-prompt context; this is materially different from ordinary task or tool policy. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: base (A) — Self-Evolution Agent decides whether the standard rare-signal criterion warrants a durable identity edit; parent (P) — authenticated operator directly owns the authoritative core-file edit and may override/undo Agent evolution. - Return-to-operation path: both modes persist the core file used by prompt construction; operator writes additionally evict the cached runtime, and later Agent initialization reloads the changed identity/policy context.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a00011ae5e8e9ee62aa713d51f283559f12102d4/assessments/cowagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a00011ae5e8e9ee62aa713d51f283559f12102d4\">a00011a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:24:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agno-agi/agno\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agno</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agno-agi/agno/tree/44219f8532e2fe6ce936850455f4b9a2567e4194\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 44219f8532e2fe6ce936850455f4b9a2567e4194\">44219f8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: member agents autonomously execute bounded model/tool work and return results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: TeamMode.tasks provides an out-of-box agent-owned mutual-adjustment loop over a shared task board. The leader assigns ownership, prevents duplicate tasks, encodes dependencies, observes member outcomes and can retry/reassign/change the plan; dependency state prevents conflicting/premature execution. This goes beyond one-shot delegation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the same leader has a current whole-team view of tasks, assignees, dependency/blocked state, results and failures, plus authority to create/reprioritize through dependencies, assign/reassign members, execute work, update status and decide overall completion. That is a concrete agent-owned current-control loop over shared operational commitments. Human-in-the-loop paths pause member/tool execution and return the result to the team; they do not independently take ownership of the team-level assignment/plan/completion right, so no separate (P) mode is published.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: Agno lets applications compose reviewer/critic members, but the standard team runtime does not supply a distinct sufficiently independent audit channel with alternative operational access and required corrective closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: memory, knowledge, reasoning and learning capabilities can improve team operation but do not by themselves instantiate a distinct external-and-prospective intelligence function coupled against S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: team role, instructions, permissions, approvals and identity are application/parent configured. The tasks leader regulates execution but is not given ultimate agent-owned policy/identity authority or an S3–S4 closure role.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9/assessments/agno.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9\">9ee1ea7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T17:08:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lose4578/Auriga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Auriga</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lose4578/Auriga/tree/3e1be75e186e49f8c268fd5fc6c08427f99254ce\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3e1be75e186e49f8c268fd5fc6c08427f99254ce\">3e1be75 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: pursue robot-manipulation goals through visual observation, task planning, multi-stage preview/execution, measured action feedback and recovery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing object/robot geometry, failed or partial grasps, occlusion, movement slips, constrained arm trajectories and uncertain action completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next physical task action, observation, program, trajectory revision or recovery in light of returned evidence. - Decision owner: primary model-driven Codex actor (named Astra in optional verification documentation), with action authority mediated by the Harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: action identity and current-observation checks, native motion planner, gripper guards, single action writer, program review broker, action ledger, wall/budget supervision and the physical simulator. - Closure path: Codex task/plan → robotobserve and robotaction preview → model selects execute → Harness.execute native worker → measured receipt/images/stop reason → model adjusts or terminates episode. - Boundary reachability: normal gateway creates the Codex session and Harness, registers robotaction and connects execution to native RoboDojo worker. The model/tool loop is shipped with documented flags, not merely a benchmark test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static robot safety gates do not select a new manipulation strategy; the actor model makes the task-level decisions even when a hard guard prevents unsafe execution. - Evidence: README execution loop, Harness, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct conflict-specific inter-S1 coordination and return is established in the supported robot actor organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dual-arm trajectory interference and concurrent program/native action requests are real actuator/resource hazards but not conflicts among distinct autonomous S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: arbitrate independent agent commitments and return a coordination resolution; not evidenced. - Decision owner: one actor plans both arms; deterministic Harness serializes physical actions and rejects stale plans, rather than an S2 coordination owner regulating competing agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stage-order dependencies, dual-arm path preview, active-writer exclusivity, plan revision, program broker approval and stop gates. - Closure path: native constraints and the primary actor can alter a manipulation task, but no separate inter-S1 interference-resolution result is fed into distinct operational agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: two robot arms are not independent goal-directed S1 cells; an optional evidence agent audits a claim but does not compete for physical task execution. - Evidence: unified actions, Harness action writer, program review. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no independent whole-organization current-control authority over a population of operational S1 commitments is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: native action budget, simulator step accounting, active action conflicts, unknown stop confirmation, model transport failures and episode wall limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select systemic allocation/priority/commitment intervention across distinct operations; absent beyond the single robot episode&#39;s work plan. - Decision owner: primary actor owns task strategy; Harness enforces state/timeout/cost constraints; operator defines task/benchmark configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: supervision.waitturn, action gate, per-action budget, program source grants, GPU resource checks and status/feedback records. - Closure path: failures can stop one episode or prompt its actor to try another action, not a separate whole-current S3 decision that regulates multiple autonomous units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: counters, stop gates and request retry policy are enforcement; removing a hypothetical S3 model leaves them intact. - Evidence: runtime supervision, Harness, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: the primary robot actor&#39;s local claim that a selected grasp/lift/transfer/place/release or retreat phase achieved its expected visible result, as distinct from a native worker&#39;s narrower completed execution receipt. - Ordinary reporting path: Harness.execute returns a native action receipt and current measured observation to the primary actor, which can then report success on the strength of its own readings; a tool response alone does not prove object retention or task completion. - Complementary access path: the separately launched Sol reviewer is given the raw linked BEFORE/AFTER permitted RGB images, measured robot state, immutable action IDs/receipts and the actor&#39;s intended outcome as a hypothesis; it checks visual identity/relation independently and returns a structured met/notmet/uncertain verdict with gaps. - Independence boundary: CodexEvidenceAgent creates a fresh ephemeral thread, empty workspace and isolated home, disables memories/tools/hooks, and rejects observed helper tool activity; it is not forked from the acting Codex history and cannot issue motion commands. The actor chooses checkpoint and evidence, limiting scope but not transferring visual judgment to the actor. - Who acts on findings: the primary Astra actor receives visionreview results in its ordinary tool stream and is instructed to resolve unmet/uncertain evidence with fresh observations or changed recovery; the reviewer itself cannot execute or compel a trajectory and the operating loop retains physical action ownership. - Function: provide an independent, evidence-limited second opinion on a manipulation operation&#39;s claimed visible outcome and send discrepancies or uncertainty back to the acting agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic success interpretations of completed motion receipts, wrong target identity, occlusion, unsupported grasp/placement/release claims and partial execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge met, notmet or uncertain for a claimed phase by comparing independent before/after RGB, execution receipts and measured robot state, and specify evidence gaps/recovery feedback. - Decision owner: separate fresh gpt-6.1-sol evidence agent; primary Astra remains owner of the subsequent physical action and the operator decides whether to enable this configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic provenance and schema checks, isolated fresh home/thread, suppressed tools/memory, payload source hashes, shared model budget, exact model assertion and unsupported-positive veto in constrainanswer. - Closure path: actor selects meaningful checkpoint and invokes visionreview(evidencemode=&#39;operation&#39;) → fresh Sol independently examines linked images/receipts and issues bounded verdict and follow-up → tool response returns to the primary actor&#39;s Codex turn → actor instructed to resolve uncertainty or choose a changed recovery before later motions. The auditor never executes robot actions. - Boundary reachability: features.visualreviewmodel=&#39;gpt-6.1-sol&#39; is a documented supported config; the actual Workbench instantiates VisualReviewer(CodexEvidenceAgent(session)), the runtime tool handler dispatches it and returns the structured assessment to the ordinary actor context. Default null mode has no S3 helper; positive vector reflects the first-party enabled operating mode, not default execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the fresh model provides the discretionary visual evidence judgment; deterministic validation limits unsupported assertions but cannot itself interpret ambiguous target retention/phase outcome. Actor authority to choose recovery is distinct from auditor judgment ownership. - Evidence: mode and return contract, Workbench construction, fresh agent isolation, review judgment, actor recovery instructions, tool handler. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: actor chooses evidence/request and need not obey a fixed compulsory verification phase sequence; this is a first-party supported optional independent second-opinion loop, not privileged simulator-ground-truth audit or guaranteed physical correction. No real provider/robot verification was executed here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate externally prospective adaptation process with authorized whole-runtime capability-choice and return is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel object geometries, repeated failed grasps, changing camera viewpoints, different robot scenes and future task transfer. - Decisive decision or feedback right: change organizational operating capability based on prospective environmental alternatives; not evidenced at the assessed one-episode recursion. - Decision owner: primary model adapts task actions in the current scene; developers/operators configure perception services, model and robot plugins. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: adaptiveactioncheckpoint failure reminders, observation memory, trajectory cost previews, program synthesis and optional external perception. - Closure path: measured failures can prompt an immediate alternate grasp, viewpoint or trajectory within one task; no distinct environment/future analysis → approved organization-wide capability adaptation → feedback loop is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adaptive recovery within one operating episode is S1 learning/action selection, not automatically S4 strategic adaptation. - Evidence: checkpoint helper, model-led recovery, unified actions/memory. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate organizational policy/identity arbitration with legitimate authoritative return is implemented inside the robot episode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: robot safety boundaries, model permissions, execution modes, privileged task truth, tool grants and physical stop decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adjudicate identity-level commitments and revise binding policy for the whole; not shown beyond fixed configuration and task controls. - Decision owner: developer/operator specifies guardrails and modes; deterministic Harness enforces validation; primary actor decides ordinary manipulation steps within those bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: actor observation redaction, action schema and state locks, program grants, model call budget and sandbox controls. - Closure path: individual actions are admitted/rejected against predefined rules, but no policy-level dispute → qualified authority → ultimate decision → new identity-governing operating rule is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may choose a risky trajectory and receive advisory data, but it does not own the system&#39;s ultimate identity or change invariant control policy. - Evidence: action gate, actor information boundary, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ec6167d00afe015e5c030fd19139e421de731e13/assessments/au/auriga.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ec6167d00afe015e5c030fd19139e421de731e13\">ec6167d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:08:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJUZhiyuCai/bread-companion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bread Companion</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJUZhiyuCai/bread-companion/tree/058ad153e27bd6470a7e5ef4cab778a93d659a6d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 058ad153e27bd6470a7e5ef4cab778a93d659a6d\">058ad15 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous bounded conversation and evidence-task transformation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different user material, missing/conflicting evidence, rejected interpretations and malformed or inappropriate generated candidates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose supported findings, wording, draft structure or a practice question; revise after concrete rejection/validation feedback. - Decision owner: the configured real-agent model within the first-party generation/repair loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mode prompts, selected source segments, schema/evidence/style validators, two-attempt repair, run IDs and client lifecycle checks. - Closure path: validation errors re-enter a new generator attempt; accepted content becomes current findings/card/practice state, influencing later turns. - Boundary reachability: real-agent.generate implements the provider interface used by the deployed server; BreadController submits a current turn and reducer applies only matching epoch/context results. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model determines substantive task content and correction, while the controller enforces validity and human save authority. Fixed UI transitions alone could not produce the same discretionary outcomes. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; prompts/agent.ts; domain/evidence.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: no general tool-executing autonomy; drafts require user confirmation for persistence. This is not a therapy efficacy or clinical safety claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct operational-unit coordination path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These are one conversational work cell&#39;s pipeline/transport stages and revision-consistency controls. No distinct purposeful operational S1 cells with an inter-S1 disturbance/attenuation relation were found. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; services/run-controller.ts; services/call-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-current organizational control function established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These regulate individual requests, versions, service capacity and task consent. A session view or stop button is not by itself whole-system resource/commitment regulation with a function-specific parent decision loop. - Evidence: services/bread-controller.ts; services/run-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts; server/limits.ts; server/admission.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: narrow complementary semantic audit of whether contested analysis reintroduces the user&#39;s denied interpretation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a candidate can pass schema/verbatim/lexical checks yet paraphrase or recommend the rejected interpretation again. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently compare actual candidate with user rejection record and judge semantic consistency. - Decision owner: the model in the fresh rejection-review invocation; runtime parses/enforces its verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate review prompt/request, only candidate/rejection/current user data, bounded audit input, conservative verdict parsing and forced retry/fallback. - Closure path: inconsistent/invalid audit adds REJECTEDINTERPRETATIONREPEATED; generator receives explicit corrective feedback; after a second repeated rejection the product returns quote/unknown fallback rather than display the contested interpretation. - Boundary reachability: RealAgentProvider invokes the separate reviewer only for non-listen analysis with rejectedFindings after normal validation; reducer REJECT and context-builder persist/send that rejection record. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the independent review call supplies semantic judgment beyond the generator&#39;s candidate and deterministic lexical check; parsing is enforcement, not the owner. No independent provider identity is claimed. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; server/providers/rejection-review.ts; domain/reducer.ts; domain/context-builder.ts; domain/evidence.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: same configured model/provider may serve both roles; independence is fresh context and task separation for this narrow consistency claim, not empirical independence from shared model biases. Ordinary chat is unaudited. - Claim being audited: the candidate respects the user&#39;s previously rejected interpretation and its paraphrases. - Ordinary reporting path: generator candidate passes schema/evidence/style and exact-normalized rejection checks. - Complementary access path: fresh semantic comparison of the actual full candidate against persisted user rejection records. - Independence boundary: separate stateless request with dedicated review instructions and no generator hidden reasoning/history; source of correction is user record, not a generator self-report. - Who acts on findings: the provider&#39;s repair loop withholds the candidate, requests revision and eventually substitutes a traceable non-interpretive fallback.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective capability-adaptation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These adjust current task content, context and service availability. They do not develop future environment/capability options and return an adaptation decision that changes the harness&#39;s present repertoire. Research/benchmark plans are adjacent. - Evidence: domain/context-builder.ts; domain/preferences.ts; server/providers/real-agent.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; docs/product-harness.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity or ultimate-policy decision/return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static identity/permissions and user authority over task/content/save do not supply ultimate-policy arbitration. No identity-level issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate decision owner and returned as a new governing policy. - Evidence: prompts/agent.ts; domain/policy.ts; domain/reducer.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; docs/product-harness.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8c0178fd561849a1d61ba41f63780227cb755b15/assessments/br/bread-companion.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8c0178fd561849a1d61ba41f63780227cb755b15\">8c0178f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:46:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stoicdataguy/asktravel-agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AskTravel Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stoicdataguy/asktravel-agent-harness/tree/9204095f29720ba70cdd90c9ccd6d214cda36c72\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9204095f29720ba70cdd90c9ccd6d214cda36c72\">9204095 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce researched trip advice and structured itineraries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: destinations, preferences, incomplete data, live tool observations and invalid itinerary arguments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose searches/playbooks, interpret observations and select/revise the itinerary. - Decision owner: the instantiated travelagent model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangChain execution, first-party tool wrappers, Postgres, retry/compaction middleware and Pydantic validation. - Closure path: tool and validation observations return to the model; presentitinerary persists and displays the chosen plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves storage/search wrappers without discretionary travel synthesis. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/api/openaicompat.py; src/travelagent/tools/itinerary.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Bounded source assessment of the supported distribution; no live model or performance claim. - Boundary reachability: API lifespan constructs buildagent; the public chat router invokes that graph with user context.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No supplied regulation of a specific inter-S1 disturbance is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel tools, independent chats and background upkeep must not be equated with coordinated autonomous work cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: thread/checkpoint branching, user namespaces, parallel tool execution and idle scheduling. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/api/threadsync.py; src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; src/travelagent/tools/memory.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-service current resource/commitment decision loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider rate limits and background workload are handled as configured runtime constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: retry backoff, capped users per maintenance cycle, API state and database persistence. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/retry.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge the travel actor&#39;s persisted user-fact claims against raw user statements outside the ordinary chat-production path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misfiled interests/dislikes, contradicted or retracted saved facts and missing user-stated preferences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether a saved user claim needs add/update/delete/noop and propose the smallest source-supported correction. - Decision owner: the separately prompted structured memory-review model invoked by llmproposer; not applyplan&#39;s deterministic matcher. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: idle-chat discovery, checkpoint access, guarded store writes, source-quote checks, sensitive-fact exclusion, MAXOPS and memorylog. - Closure path: the reviewer proposes corrections; accepted operations update the shared fact store, and graph.systemprompt reloads that store on subsequent model calls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the reviewer the scheduler and quote checker cannot decide semantic reclassification or contradiction; they only constrain/apply its proposed judgment. - Evidence: src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; src/travelagent/tools/memory.py; src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; tests/testconsolidation.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: A narrowly scoped source-supported memory-claim audit, not travel-answer verification. Same provider is permitted; trust is limited by selected recent messages, saved-fact evidence, model error, quote matching and excluded sensitive fields. No measured audit effectiveness. - Claim being audited: persisted user facts written by ordinary savememory calls faithfully represent user preferences/statements. - Ordinary reporting path: the travel actor writes summarized facts through savememory and later consumes their prompt projection. - Complementary access path: consolidateuser reads HumanMessage content directly from checkpoints and compares it to the store in a fresh structured review context. - Independence boundary: the producing chat actor neither selects the idle review invocation nor supplies its ordinary narrative as sole evidence; separate reviewer prompt and raw user messages can challenge that actor&#39;s stored claim. Shared provider/store do not establish global independence or coverage. - Who acts on findings: guarded applyplan updates memory and logs before/after; subsequent travel calls reread corrected facts. - Boundary reachability: app.lifespan creates the nonstreaming proposer and runconsolidationloop when default memoryconsolidation is enabled; disabled mode intentionally has no audit run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environment-to-capability adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: live travel availability and changed user preferences are current-task/domain data. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: search tools, skill playbooks, memory consolidation and dynamic prompt refresh. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/prompts.py; src/travelagent/skills/init.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; docs/PLAN.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime ultimate-policy or identity decision-and-return path is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task preferences and data controls are not identity-level organizational disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system guidelines, sensitive-memory guard, settings and user export/delete endpoints. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/prompts.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5ae1eb582c8453ba6a02b1827749d624be95ba28/assessments/as/asktravel-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5ae1eb582c8453ba6a02b1827749d624be95ba28\">5ae1eb5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"3\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T16:44:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eust-w/opengeniemaster\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenGenieMaster</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>3</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eust-w/opengeniemaster/tree/8f6dab22a54a318bf8afa221269a6f9c00fa860c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8f6dab22a54a318bf8afa221269a6f9c00fa860c\">8f6dab2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: primary embodied domain work (e.g. inspect/restock, retrieve, clean, handoff) by agent-guided robot work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user task variation, unavailable/unknown objects, visual occlusion, robot capability limits, unexpected failures, interruptions and contradictory task outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the in-process Claude coding agent chooses task decomposition, relevant tools, guidance, execution/recovery/handoff and whether to retry, based on live task context and returned observations. - Decision owner: ClaudeAgent in the optional first-party ogm agent mode; individual robot policy owns lower-level execution, not the higher-level coding-agent choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolRegistry, EEP schema, Harness, domain templates, adapters, admission interlocks, task store, completion criteria, typed failure/recovery hints, bounded turn count. - Closure path: goal → model tool decision → Harness.execute/robot → observation, checked outcome and persisted continuity → next model turn or terminal response. - Boundary reachability: documented ogm agent command constructs ClaudeAgent with first-party operator prompt and directly wired EEP tools; optional Anthropic SDK/API availability is an execution prerequisite. It is not necessary to invent an agent in application code. Offline fake-client tests demonstrate tool-loop wiring, not actual paid-model behaviour. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing ClaudeAgent leaves ScriptedOrchestrator, which can follow fixed recovery rules but does not retain the agent&#39;s open-ended choice of guidance and recovery under novel user contexts. S1 A belongs narrowly to the shipped optional Claude mode; the deterministic demo does not itself establish A. - Evidence: Claude tool loop, operator instructions, EEP handlers, execution/feedback, CLI agent entrypoint. - Basis: first-party optional agent tool loop with consequential environment feedback. - Confidence: high for code-level reachability, moderate for deployed agent outcomes (no provider E2E run). - Caveats: robot simulator is semantic; physical policy autonomy and real-world safety are not verified; provider model behaviour was not exercised.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: dampen conflicts between distinct embodied S1 work cells over capacity, shared work-item affinity and interrupted handoffs. - Distinct S1 units: two or more separately assigned robot/policy work cells with their own capability and physical state in a fleet; not two names for the same Claude agent. - Inter-S1 disturbance: assigning one busy robot simultaneously, transferring an unfinished pick–carry–place group while the first robot still holds its object, running dependent steps before prerequisites, or handing off to an incapable robot. - Attenuating coordination relation: Harness.dispatch excludes busy/stopped units, respects group affinity and dependencies; updatetask(assign/handoff) mediates exceptional reassignment. The agent&#39;s operating prompt directs failure-driven handoff and deliberate retry/wait/stop decisions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: assignment and handoff update persistent TaskStore; the next eeptaskget / eepdispatch / execution uses the new robot, group and dependency state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the code explicitly detects the inter-robot work-item/capacity conflict and blocks/moves conflicting assignments, including held-object restart semantics; a simple shared task list would not do so. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent incompatible robot assignments and unstable cross-robot handoff in shared work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the autonomous Claude agent can choose to wait, hand off/reassign, force-restart affected work items, or route around failures based on robot feedback; deterministic dispatch enforces ordinary constraints but does not itself own discretionary S2 judgment. - Decision owner: ClaudeAgent for exception coordination in the supported multi-robot mode; deterministic Harness for bound-preserving enforcement. Routine default dispatch alone would support only constructor/enforcement, not A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: busy-robot set, capability filtering, group binding, held-object tracking, dependency graph, continuity persistence, readiness checks. - Closure path: two robot work cells create assignment/holding conflict → feedback/continuity visible through EEP → agent chooses handoff/retry/alternate robot → guard updates whole work item → subsequent dispatch/execution observes new ownership. - Boundary reachability: multi-robot scenarios and eeptaskupdate handoff are first-party tools available directly to ClaudeAgent; no custom coordinator is required in that mode. S2 A does not claim the scripted dispatcher has autonomous organizational discretion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the full exception-response choice can be made by the tool-using model; deterministic locks/guards are supporting regulators. Removing the model would retain fixed handoff mechanics but not its context-sensitive coordination discretion. - Evidence: operating prompt, dispatch and busy rules, handoff safeguards, continuity group assignment, handoff regression. - Basis: identifiable multi-S1 interference, conflict-specific guard, model-accessible discretionary recovery and returned assignment state. - Confidence: moderate: source and synthetic test wiring are clear; real multi-robot model behavior is untested. - Caveats: if physical robots are only passive actuators under one central actor, the organizational S2 interpretation narrows; standard simulated policy work cells still retain local execution environments and separate assignments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: provide a current whole-fleet task/priority/resource regulation path, beyond local per-step delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous active tasks competing for finite robot availability, differing priorities, stopped robots and unmet commitments. - Whole-system current view: eeptasklist lists all tasks, eepcapabilities lists fleet skills/status, Harness.busyrobots spans active, paused and interrupted tasks, and ScriptedOrchestrator.runready enumerates all active tasks. - Current-control decision scope: assign resources across all ready tasks, use task priority when selecting the next work item, stop/pause or restart commitments and intervene on assignment. Task priority is chosen at creation, not autonomously renegotiated by a dedicated whole-system governor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a developer/composed operator or future first-party agent policy must decide whole-system priorities, commitments and exceptions; the shipped scheduler executes preselected priorities and capacity rules. - Decision owner: no autonomous agent demonstrably assigned sustained whole-system S3 discretion in the standard operating instructions. The caller chooses priorities and when the scheduler runs; scripts enforce them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: priority-sorted task store, cross-task dispatch, runready highest-priority selection, stop/resume and event log. - Closure path: all-task view + priorities → scheduled fleet assignment/intervention → persistent task/robot state → next scheduling pass. The first-party constructor is real; an autonomous whole-system deciding/feedback actor must still be composed. - Boundary reachability: eeptasklist, priority at eeptaskcreate, cross-task eepdispatch and ScriptedOrchestrator.runready are shipped callable modes. No private benchmark oracle is needed. This is a function-specific S3 path, not mere generic tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scripted runready orders work by caller-supplied priority; Claude&#39;s documented ogm agent goal prompt serves one task and does not establish an autonomous sustained fleet-wide current-control review. Enforcement is not ownership. Human eeptaskupdate calls are local operations, not a distinct operationally closed parent S3 mode. - Evidence: all-task and fleet view, task priorities and dispatch, cross-task busy guard, scheduler runready, tool registry. - Basis: shipped S3-specific whole-current-task capacity/priority constructor with missing autonomous decisive S3 policy. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: no operator-governed (P) whole-system governance closure, budget bargaining, or accountable strategic priorities is demonstrated.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge the robot policy&#39;s own success report and the ordinary step-completion record through a separately callable, more direct evidence check. - Claim being audited: the physical action succeeded and its effect persists (e.g. an item really remains in the intended slot, not just policy-reported success). - Ordinary reporting path: robot adapter execute returns a policy success/outcome and observation; harness evaluates immediate criteria, records execution and updates task status. - Complementary access path: eepverify / recheckstep later calls criterion checks, optionally at close range, through the adapter&#39;s separate check operation; contradictory results mark prior execution records, convert succeeded steps to failed, and reopen completed tasks. - Independence boundary: the verification request and criterion signal are separate from the policy&#39;s initial self-reported success, and can overturn it in the shipped simulator. However physical HttpAdapter.check calls the same configured robot service (/check), with no guaranteed independent detector/operator/credential ownership; the model can also invoke the check itself. This is an explicit complementary constructor, not a demonstrated organizationally independent S3 auditor. - Who acts on findings: the harness reconciles continuity deterministically, and the coding agent can read discrepancies, recover or create new memory; a distinct audit owner/trigger/sampling authority is not supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false-positive policy completion, persistent-effect regressions and false negatives hidden in routine execution summaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independent auditor&#39;s selection of when/how to check and corrective judgment is not operationally assigned; the first-party alternative-evidence and contradiction-to-rework feedback path is implemented. - Decision owner: needs first-party-mode integration/developer composition for independent S3 authority; Harness.recheckstep owns deterministic reconciliation, not independent audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: criteria API, alternate camera/close-range flag, contradiction records, task reopening and offline regression tests. - Closure path: policy claim → later direct criterion check → contradiction/reopening and marked evidence → S1 rework and future reflection. Distinct auditor authority is the missing component for A. - Boundary reachability: eepverify is exposed to the built-in Claude and MCP agents and Harness.recheckstep is shipped in the runtime; this is more than a test-only audit, though real detector independence is not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a same-loop verifier can provide valuable extra evidence but does not prove a separately controlled independent audit; C recognizes the material constructor without fabricating A. - Evidence: recheck and task reopening, robot adapter direct check, tool eepverify, regression showing overturned completion, criteria. - Basis: separate criterion and corrective-return path, conditional independence at the evidence level, missing independent audit ownership for A. - Confidence: moderate; stronger simulation path than unverified real backend. - Caveats: same backend may correlate failures; normal immediate completion-criterion checks alone would not suffice for S3.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: expose a capability-adaptation path using observed environmental difficulty and prospective targeted rehearsal, then return learned alternative guidance/coordination into subsequent work. - External distinction: repeated physical-world failures in particular robot/model/skill/object contexts, e.g. occluded targets, look-alike stock, changed item placements and slow handling, compared across execution records and domain catalog. - Future / prospective distinction: capability-gap severity and planned idle-time practice for troublesome contexts before the next customer/work task; this is an opportunity to develop/rehearse future response capacity, not just retry the current failed step. - Adaptation option generated: capabilitygaps groups recent failures, relative attempts and slowness; planpractice generates low-priority repeated pick/return tasks; RuleReflector or optional LLMReflector mines successful recoveries and contradictions into contextual patch instructions. - Path back into current capability / S3: practice runs produce execution records → evidence-checked SharedAdmission admits or rejects guidance/recovery/criteria/coordination patches → GuidanceBuilder and chooserobot apply relevant memory on later dispatch/execution; weak non-safety memory can be retired based on outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: foreseeable performance deterioration or new capability demands in observed external object/robot contexts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: whether and when to run future-oriented practice, what capacity to spend, which gap to prioritize, and whether to make this a standing adaptation program. eepcapabilitygaps(schedulepractice=true) is callable, but no automatic idle/foresight owner and no sustained whole-system adaptation conversation with S3 are assigned in the standard runtime. - Decision owner: caller/developer must compose the autonomous S4 agenda and authority; deterministic gap ranking/practice creation and evidence-gated memory support do not by themselves own prospective choice. An optional Claude agent could call the constructor, but the shipped operator prompt lacks a recurring future-environment scan/idle planning loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution record history, contextual failure aggregation, low-priority practice workflow, rule/LLM reflection, schema/evidence/safety admission, versioned long-term memory and replay tests. - Closure path: environmental difficulty → exposed gap and practice option → caller-scheduled rehearsal → feedback/reflection/admitted patch → changed future guidance/robot preference. The function-specific construction path exists, but autonomous S4 initiative remains to be composed. - Boundary reachability: eepcapabilitygaps with schedulepractice and memory tools are actually in the shipped tool registry, not just a README roadmap; rule reflection and admitted patches run in standard simulation and the MCP mode. Physical extrapolation not claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a reflective memory loop alone is not S4. The distinct prospective practice option and future capability return make this more than generic memory, while lack of a scheduled autonomous environmental/adaptation governor keeps it at C. - Evidence: capability-gap/practice construction, tool exposure, reflection, admission safety/evidence, memory application, architecture memory path. - Basis: first-party prospective gap/practice-to-later-capability constructor; no complete autonomous S4 decision loop or parent mode. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: gaps are mined from internal execution records of externally situated work, not broad market/strategy sensing. There is no robot-weight self-training; flywheel.py stops at dataset export.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: would decide or revise ultimate identity/mission and balance current robot operations against the future adaptation program. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unresolved mission/safety/strategic-policy tensions beyond routine robot-task control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party runtime path accepts a genuinely ultimate-policy question, routes it to legitimate authority, records an authoritative identity decision and returns it to govern subsequent operation. - Decision owner: none evidenced for an active S5 loop; hard-coded safety constraints and user task goals do not constitute an identity governor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: domain safety rules, SharedAdmission rejection of unsafe guidance/memory, robot stop/resume, current-model prompt, human teleoperation example, repo contributor governance. - Closure path: not established at ultimate-policy level; operational stop/resume and evidence-based memory patches affect tasks or safety settings rather than deciding identity or arbitrating S3–S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent cannot revise ultimate mission under a dedicated first-party process, and no first-party parent institution/human S5 escalation-return path is supplied. Accepting task goals and protecting immutable safety flags are ordinary operating constraints. - Evidence: operator prompt/safety, admission rules, memory admission cannot weaken safety, robot stop/resume, human backend, research vs repo identity note. - Basis: bounded negative conclusion for reviewed runtime rather than universal impossibility. - Confidence: moderate-high within shipped modes. - Caveats: a real deployment may have human/organizational identity governance outside this repository, but that external authority cannot be inferred here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1c021299b02811b5e34a4b01833cdba494fca0b3/assessments/op/opengeniemaster.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1c021299b02811b5e34a4b01833cdba494fca0b3\">1c02129 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T23:02:10+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sgeos/pydromon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pydromon</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sgeos/pydromon/tree/65671672b812b1d4c88f8e7633fdd3a1e50bb5f0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 65671672b812b1d4c88f8e7633fdd3a1e50bb5f0\">6567167 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Configured session lines produce project work and exchange results through their first-party daemon composition. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User tasks, local workspace changes, messages, background runs and engine/tool failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose local tools, communications, work actions and whether to continue or end a turn. - Decision owner: The model-backed session-line actor; external Claude/Codex/Pi engines supply inference/tool-loop dependencies rather than autonomous Dromon metasystem judgments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party driver construction, tool exposure, mailbox/event log, wake scheduler, sandbox/settings composition and persisted engine sessions. - Closure path: Queued mail/wake → line prompt and configured engine → chosen tools/messages/work → recorded turn outcomes → later line turns. - Boundary reachability: The distributed daemon creates configured session lines with first-party Dromon tools and driver/engine wiring. The supported Claude/Codex-backed composition is executable without implementing a new harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model-backed line actors, the daemon transports messages and enforces policy but cannot choose meaningful project work. Dromon does not receive credit for unrelated engine features. - Evidence: [Scheduler][R1], [engine composition][R2], [daemon][R3], [line configuration][R4], [README][R5]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No live subscribed engine or paid model was run; engine independence and operational quality are not claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Declared resource leases and role-seat ownership regulate concrete shared-resource/write collisions among operational lines. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two active lines may try to use the same named project resource; role seats sharing a worktree must avoid competing writers and stale ownership transitions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A line chooses when to acquire/release a resource lease and can hand off or recover role ownership subject to observed eligibility. - Decision owner: Configured agent lines own invocation/release/handoff choices; the operator declares permitted resource names/role terms, and deterministic state validates exclusivity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Lease holder/expiry events, refusal with holder/expiration feedback, non-renewal/TTL, role owner invariants, engine access rebuilding and journaled handoff. - Closure path: Contender calls acquire → receives grant or current-holder/expiry refusal → adjusts later resource use; release/expiry enables another acquisition. Role handoff records a note, transfers authority, wakes the successor and rebuilds the former owner&#39;s access. - Boundary reachability: acquirelease/releaselease and seat ownership tools are supplied to running lines by Scheduler; project-config leases/roles enable the shipped path, not a custom conflict resolver. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The actors decide when to reserve/release/pass work; the log and guards enforce those decisions. This credits that bounded agent-mediated coordination mode, not generic messaging or the scheduler itself. - Evidence: [Lease tool and role tool implementations][R1], [role invariants/one-writer boundary][R6], [project enablement][R7], [line tool semantics][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Resource leases are cooperative: unmanaged external processes or ignored lease conventions are not forcibly excluded. Deterministic automatic failover is support, not autonomous discretion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: The project operator regulates the whole current set of session lines, role capacity, budgets and continuing commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Runaway autonomous turns/cost, held or failed lines, exhausted provider plans, conflicting current staffing and engine availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review project-wide state and choose line/role/budget/hibernation/rebind/interruption/release changes or answer a held line. - Decision owner: The authenticated local project operator, acting as the legitimate parent of the project run. No first-party autonomous whole-project controller is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Private operator socket, all-line status summaries, durable events, operator-attributed messages, hold notices/question queue, CLI commands and scheduler/engine enforcement. - Closure path: Whole-project status or held-line notice → operator current-control choice → daemon command/event → scheduler applies limits, ownership, model, hold or interruption changes → subsequent turns run under the returned decision. - Boundary reachability: Daemon.execute serves operator-authority status/settings/role/budget/configure/release/interrupt commands, and status includes every line. The first-party scheduler routes hold questions and operator answers back into later operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the operator leaves fixed limits and failover machinery, not discretionary whole-system resource/commitment revision. P describes the supplied supervised mode; enforcement does not become A or C. - Evidence: [Operator protocol dispatch/current status][R3], [holds and operator-answer scheduler][R1], [project limits/roles][R7], [role runtime updates][R6], [client protocol][R8]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The parent boundary is this local project, not the public OSS organization; operator socket access is an explicit trust assumption.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No installed complementary independent audit judgment-and-corrective-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect work-completion claims, stale notes, disclosure mistakes and engine-reporting errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separate audit-specific actor or constructor adjudicates operational truth and returns corrective control. - Decision owner: Lines report their work; runtime observes process/session/log facts. Read-only seats are configured by users without a supplied audit workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Daemon-stamped commit hashes, role journals, activity/event logs, raw transport evidence/replay and disclosure checks. - Closure path: Facts can be inspected by an operator or peer; the standard product has no audit verdict/corrective-action protocol for a particular completion claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent fact capture is useful evidence but is not itself discretionary complementary audit; tests, developer reviews and optional advisory seats do not supply an installed audit function. - Evidence: [Scheduler status/journal/check tools][R1], [engine transport evidence][R2], [evidence store][R9], [role design][R6], [line tools][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A user can organize reviewer lines through general messages; that application would need separate function/independence/closure evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider budget changes, new model choices and suggested project patches are available current inputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped intelligence actor develops future-oriented capability options and closes their return into current capability. - Decision owner: Operator chooses reconfiguration/upgrades; ordinary lines may submit untrusted patch proposals. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: proposechange storage, project reload/rebind, provider-budget failover and optional local teacher lifecycle. - Closure path: Proposals are explicitly never applied by Dromon; teacher code only loads/unloads a local model. Rebinding/failover responds to present availability rather than prospective adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A future roadmap, a model called teacher, context handoff or a current capacity switch is not S4. Generic operator patch handling does not create a function-specific parent adaptation loop. - Evidence: [Proposal no-apply contract][R10], [teacher boundary][R11], [role failover][R6], [reload/rebind][R3]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Repository-development/dogfood decisions and future Rust/Keleusma ports are adjacent.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No function-specific identity/ultimate-policy decision-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Access/disclosure rules, operator messages, project instructions and role terms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identity-level issue is specifically adjudicated and returned by ultimate authority as a new governing policy. - Decision owner: Operator configures the project and its permission/disclosure boundaries; agents have explicitly bounded local tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Operator/line address attribution, private socket trust, disclosure checks, tool/recipient policies, protected state and role rules. - Closure path: Configuration and operator answers alter current execution, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue/decision protocol or S3–S4 balance closure is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator is a real parent for S3 current control; that does not promote all configuration or question replies to S5. - Evidence: [Operator and line authority split][R3], [line tools/policy][R4], [project configuration][R7], [scheduler trust and question semantics][R1]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: First-party development documents record human design decisions, but those are not an installed policy actor in an arbitrary user project.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b8dbf77260552d46200e82343dce1fc8cd20a3db/assessments/py/pydromon.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b8dbf77260552d46200e82343dce1fc8cd20a3db\">b8dbf77 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T21:09:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nagisanzenin/INIT-Agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">INIT-Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nagisanzenin/INIT-Agent/tree/1b8b407d2716739a4cfaff6a1751c2f968425edb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1b8b407d2716739a4cfaff6a1751c2f968425edb\">1b8b407 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Convert coding requests into tool-mediated work, revise actions after observed outcomes and return results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing code/task state, model results, tool failures, user steering and interrupted sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select subsequent code/tool actions and responses from the model&#39;s observations. - Decision owner: Mutable agent-body model in the normal supported session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model I/O types, tool dispatcher, context/log, kernel process lifecycle, Root sandbox and egress proxy. - Closure path: Input/context → model-selected tool call → dispatched tool result in the conversation → next model decision. - Boundary reachability: The installed init-agent launches the shipped body loop through the kernel; tool use is not borrowed from the provider&#39;s internal agent service. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party model chooses substantive tool use and adjusts after feedback; Root only constrains execution and supplies transport. - Evidence: seed loop, model tool-call types, tool dispatcher, body boot. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection only; no paid provider or installed-session E2E execution was performed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately evidenced first-party inter-S1 interference attenuation loop at this chosen recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential interference among multiple independent S1 operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific inter-S1 conflict-resolution right established. - Decision owner: None evidenced for S2 in the normal distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded delegation, serial body turn execution, process/sandbox separation and ordinary result return. - Closure path: Delegation and result reporting support S1 task decomposition, without an evidenced S2-specific coordination correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Multiple processes and a delegate tool do not independently demonstrate an interference/oscillation witness and subsequent coordination feedback. - Evidence: delegate implementation, delegate tool, system architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This does not assert that a custom future fleet cannot implement S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Parent-controlled current recovery and runtime constraint changes over the one live agent-and-Root system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe operation, health regressions, continued erroneous work, spend/exposure concerns and failed self-changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect system status and select owner panic/stop/recovery/rollback rather than simply accept the current run. - Decision owner: Legitimate human operator via the Root&#39;s owner channel; Root executes the returned choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Root status snapshot, owner socket/CLI, signed lineage, sandbox, watchdog and snapshot restore. - Closure path: Root current status and warnings → owner chooses panic, stop, resume or rollback → Root changes currently permitted execution/recovered tuple. - Boundary reachability: init-root status, attach, panic, resume and Root recovery are supported installed owner commands, not contributor CI or a suggested external management plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The parent selects consequential whole-system intervention; deterministic Root enforcement and autonomous threshold rollback alone are not credited as agent-owned S3 discretion. - Whole-system current view: Root publishes live slot/mode/level/charter, process state, spend, snapshots and fault/lineage status for the deployed system. - Current-control decision scope: Stop or resume current work and restore/restrict the active body state and access envelope. - Evidence: Root owner CLI, Root contract, README controls. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Parent path is local owner control, not an autonomous multi-agent S3 or organizational governance of repository contributors.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: A Root-sealed, agent-inaccessible regression check of evolved bodies is present; its ownership as the Methodology&#39;s discretionary complementary S3 audit remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Self-reported successful mutation concealing functional regression or benchmark gaming. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Challenge the current body with unseen tasks and return regression/rollback decisions. - Decision owner: Sealed deterministic Root evaluator executes tests and thresholds; no separately evidenced autonomous discretionary audit judge is identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Root-only sealed task store, canaries, paired comparisons, noise floors, signed audit records and rollback mechanism. - Closure path: Sealed task results → deterministic regression verdict → rollback/demotion or accepted audited pointer, affecting subsequent body operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Complementary first-party access and correction exist, but the recorded judge is deterministic enforcement. Further bounded analysis is needed to decide whether the established function&#39;s published ownership is C, — or unresolved under Methodology 0.3.7. - Evidence: Root sealed evaluation contract, Root module wiring, promotion gate contract. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: low for ownership, high for shipped sealed infrastructure. - Caveats: This is not an independent reviewer run or proof of successful model performance.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Propose and deploy changes to the agent&#39;s future operating capabilities from observed task/tool limitations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing requirements, repeated tool failures, inefficiencies and capability mismatch with prospective work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model chooses edits to its own prompt, extension/tool set or compiled body and requests their promotion. - Decision owner: Agent model authors the adaptation option; Root/kernel safety and performance gates may reject it without owning option generation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Editable body/extension worktree, initctl promote, G0–G4 tests, staged process hot-swap, lineage and safety rollback. - Closure path: Observed limitations → agent-authored source/extension change → promotion/gates → accepted live body/tool capability used on later turns; rejected change returns evidence for another attempt. - Boundary reachability: Self-edit is a documented native agent capability; released kernel/Root perform the promotion and can preserve conversation state through a core swap. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The source modification and future option are model-selected; a fixed compiler/test/holdout guard has veto power but does not independently choose the proposed enhancement. - External distinction: Performance and tool demands of tasks/environment relative to present agent capabilities. - Future / prospective distinction: Revised future tool/core behavior, not merely saving a current conversation. - Adaptation option generated: New/modified skills, tools and Rust body code. - Path back into current capability / S3: Accepted promotion changes the running body; Root can roll it back based on current outcome. - Evidence: README self-evolution workflow, agent evolution contract, kernel gate contract, extension authoring. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Shipped self-edit closure is not a claim of measured net capability gain; the README explicitly notes that persistent benefit is not yet demonstrated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Keep ultimate authority over the agent&#39;s identity and non-editable safety/extension charter outside the self-modifying actor. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent efforts to redefine its own authority, protected access and evolution permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select and explicitly apply the Root charter defining the owner&#39;s non-negotiable authority envelope. - Decision owner: Human owner; the independent Root validates, signs and enforces the choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Owner-only charter.toml, charter checks/digest, active charter state, Root sandbox/permission rules and separation from the agent zone. - Closure path: Owner changes charter and invokes charter check --apply → Root validates/activates it → the next runtime decisions are constrained by the new authoritative charter. - Boundary reachability: The installed Root CLI exposes owner-only charter check/apply while the mutable agent is sandboxed away from Root home. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The self-modifying agent does not control the protected charter or Root. Runtime permission requests alone were not counted; the evidenced claim is the explicit higher-level charter authority and return to operation. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Whether and under which hard non-editable constraints the agent may alter itself, gain access or survive a regression. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Human owner only; Root records/enforces the decision. - Return-to-operation path: Active charter affects subsequent agent-zone sandbox and promotion operations. - Evidence: Root charter implementation, owner apply CLI, Root architecture. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Does not infer project-level S5 governance from the existence of maintainers, contributors or release approvals.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/061d70e295c9fa9b3138e08a1b5767c5344709f1/assessments/in/init-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 061d70e295c9fa9b3138e08a1b5767c5344709f1\">061d70e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:56:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/S0ra233/Sora-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sora Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/S0ra233/Sora-Harness/tree/cbfddafca44ff18f80181943ce17ba1d271ed7a6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cbfddafca44ff18f80181943ce17ba1d271ed7a6\">cbfddaf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce local tool-mediated task results, translations and project artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: input formats, provider/tool errors, partial results, project state and user steering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose operations/tools and revise task work after observed results. - Decision owner: the model-backed main actor and supported child actors in their respective task scopes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentLoop, provider backends, tool registry, workspace restrictions, step/call limits, checkpoints and subprocess execution. - Closure path: tool results append to model history; errors or partial state lead to revised actions, while terminal results are persisted. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actors leaves wrappers/storage/guards without discretionary problem solving. - Evidence: desktop/runtime/agent/loop.py; desktop/application/conversations.py; desktop/runtime/subagents/runner.py; desktop/tools/registry.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Bounded source assessment of the supported distribution; no live model or performance claim. - Boundary reachability: ConversationApplication invokes the first-party AgentLoop; the child runner constructs that same application and sends/resumes a message.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent logical and physical write interference between concurrent parent/child operational tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workers sharing a project can overwrite the same target or publish partial/inconsistent translation packages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose disjoint work/target-ID ranges and separate result paths, revise overlapping scopes and decide serialized final publication. - Decision owner: the main model actor following the supplied subagent/tool and collaboration mode; locks only enforce exclusive writes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoped subagent tools, actual child status/results, send/continue messages, shared mutation-lock propagation and file-lock serialization. - Closure path: the main actor reads actual results, checks missing/duplicate/out-of-range IDs and file contents, corrects directly or sends revised child instructions, then commits the reconciled package serially. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the main actor a lock can serialize two incompatible edits but cannot choose semantically disjoint task boundaries or reconcile their outputs. - Evidence: desktop/plugins/subagent/tools.py; desktop/application/subagents.py; desktop/runtime/subagents/runner.py; desktop/application/mutationgate.py; desktop/tools/registry.py; desktop/plugincatalog.py; desktop/application/skills.py; skills/game-translation/references/collaboration.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Optional enabled multi-actor mode, not every single-agent chat. Configured child models/profiles are normal deployment inputs. The lock alone would not justify A. - Distinct S1 units: main task actor and independent-history child translation, review or research task actors with separate outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: shared-workspace writes, conflicting edits to the same target and overlapping or incomplete target-ID coverage. - Attenuating coordination relation: agent-selected non-overlapping ranges/result files, central serial apply/build and corrective scope messages, backed by cross-process write exclusion. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: real child states and artifact coverage checks cause corrected delegation/continuation or revised publication decisions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: first-party tool instructions and collaboration rules explicitly identify shared-file/logical collisions and complete-ID publication, rather than merely exposing a generic mailbox. - Boundary reachability: enabled subagent profiles bind real tool capabilities in SubagentManager; the built-in tool description supplies coordination rules to the model. The translation plugin&#39;s skill root and SkillService make the concrete disjoint-ID/serial-commit workflow available in the supported product mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No supplied whole-application discretionary current-control function is established beyond bounded task coordination. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child runtime limits, failed work and local task progression. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent status/cancel/continue, per-role step/time/call limits, run records and main-agent task aggregation. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: desktop/application/subagentprofiles.py; desktop/application/subagents.py; desktop/plugins/subagent/tools.py; desktop/runtime/runs.py; skills/game-translation/references/collaboration.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No material independent complementary audit path is supplied beyond ordinary operational review. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete child claims and translation/schema errors are checked in the production workflow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: actual-file reads, acceptance reports, review subtasks, tool receipts and run/checkpoint logs. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: skills/game-translation/references/collaboration.md; skills/game-translation/references/candidate-review.md; skills/game-translation/SKILL.md; desktop/plugins/subagent/tools.py; desktop/runtime/session.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Independent histories and raw-file availability alone do not turn every review into S3. This is bounded to the supplied product/skill modes, not a prohibition on a custom audit design.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environment-to-capability adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported game layouts, context limits and task-relevant reference information. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plugin/skill loading, retrieval, compaction and project-local adapter authoring. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: skills/game-translation/references/custom-adapters.md; skills/game-translation/SKILL.md; desktop/application/skills.py; desktop/runtime/context/compaction.py; desktop/plugincatalog.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication-return path is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary user task scope, prompt presets, permissions and plugin enablement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompt configuration, user questions/steering, per-profile instructions and tool allowlists. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: desktop/prompts/config.py; desktop/prompts/assembly.py; desktop/application/subagentprofiles.py; desktop/tools/contracts.py; skills/game-translation/SKILL.md; README.en.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/de3f3196a0c3cd85133074d47477e2fddede7f68/assessments/so/sora-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit de3f3196a0c3cd85133074d47477e2fddede7f68\">de3f319 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T00:17:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/quqxui/RSI-Flow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RSI-Flow</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/quqxui/RSI-Flow/tree/0f87c2b198f22e5697853cfc6d0bf2d002e48ca7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0f87c2b198f22e5697853cfc6d0bf2d002e48ca7\">0f87c2b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: A configured Pi/DeepSeek agent performs the substantive target task inside a first-party versioned workflow session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task instructions, tool/workspace state, model observations and failed subtasks under an operator-defined experiment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model chooses its response/action within an exposed task turn; results and tool feedback inform subsequent turns where the selected SDK supports them. - Decision owner: The model-backed target agent operating through the native driver; the engine and experiment runner do not choose its per-task response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Native SDK bridge, workspace/runtime snapshots, bounded prompt interface, execution timeout, credentials, receipts and result parsing. - Closure path: Input task and versioned target artifact → native model-backed agent turn → task/tool result → next agent turn and final response/experiment feedback. - Boundary reachability: Native integration is shipped under integrations/native/, wired to Pi/DeepSeek adapters and documented as supported task execution, rather than only a test mock. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model decides task behavior; removing the native model leaves an experiment runner capable of testing artifacts, not autonomous task judgment. - Evidence: native worker, Pi driver, DeepSeek driver, README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Task-level S1 depends on the configured native agent/provider; built-in offline demonstration is not proof of model autonomy or real-provider success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party anti-interference coordination among distinct task-performing S1 cells is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent candidate trials could contend for resources or contaminate comparisons. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The framework bounds parallel work and separates trial artifacts; no inter-S1 conflict-specific adjustment is evidenced. - Decision owner: Preselected execution protocol and deterministic trial harness rather than an autonomous coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task batching, trial directories, stable task IDs, budget reservations and timeouts. - Closure path: Runner results are gathered into comparison; no disturbance-detecting cross-agent coordination decision returns to alter their working relationships. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Isolation and scheduling protect tests but are not the S2-specific regulation of interactions between ongoing autonomous S1 units. - Evidence: validation stage, budget ledger, native runner. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Different target frameworks may implement their own coordination; those internals are outside this review.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No agent-owned or specifically parent-governed whole-system discretionary control of concurrent current S1 priorities and commitments is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Budget exhaustion, failed trials, protocol violations, incomplete receipts and target-version conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Acceptance thresholds and stop/rollback predicates are fixed by operator-selected protocol; these do not choose new organizational priorities in a current whole-system view. - Decision owner: Human configuration supplies constraints, deterministic engine enforces them; no in-run S3 discretion is identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BudgetLedger, SingleRoundSearch, decide, deploy, pause/resume/cancel/recover and process isolation. - Closure path: Status and budget checks deterministically stop, reject or deploy a version; the engine does not revise commitments among distinct ongoing S1 operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An operational gate and rollback authority do not become managerial judgment merely because they have effect on execution. - Evidence: engine, selection, adapters. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A custom SearchPolicy may implement additional control but no such standard autonomous whole-system owner is wired.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit of S1 claims with an audit-owned corrective return is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False task success, inaccurate version-loaded claims and unverifiable experiments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Evaluator, outcome receipts and load verifier check protocol predicates but do not exercise separate complementary organizational audit judgment. - Decision owner: The same experiment engine owns ordinary comparison/admission; no independently authorized auditor is composed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Paired evaluation, task receipts, exact-version digest, load verification, report evidence and regression thresholds. - Closure path: Comparison flows into ordinary Stage 6 acceptance; failed load checks trigger procedural rollback, not an independent audit finding sent to a corrective S3 owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Test comparison and defensive proof of load are valuable controls, but routine QA is not necessarily an independent complementary S3 challenge. - Evidence: validation, selection, reports. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: An operator can independently inspect raw receipts, but that is not an evidenced first-party audit-return mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: A configured reasoning agent generates prospective changes to the target agent&#39;s prompt/workflow from accumulated experience and diagnosed limitations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Recurring task failures, observed regressions, changes in future task mix and limits of the deployed artifact. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose candidate artifact changes, hypothesis and expected benefit, or explicitly propose no change; a subsequent protocol governs acceptance. - Decision owner: The configured model-backed CommandProposer (including the Codex integration) chooses a future adaptation option; the human-proposal mode separately supplies offline input but does not erase the supported autonomous path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Structured diagnosis, proposal context, key allowlist, versioned artifact build, baseline/candidate comparison, decide and verified deploy. - Closure path: Historical experience → diagnosis → reasoning backend chooses prospective artifact modifications → constrained build and paired evaluation → accepted new deployed artifact changes later target-agent sessions. - Boundary reachability: Engine.proposer selects command reasoning from config and Stage 4 invokes proposer.propose; the returned candidate flows into supported Stage 5 and Stage 6 deployment, not merely into documentation. - External distinction: Imported version-labelled task outcomes and feedback characterize target performance beyond the current agent turn. - Future / prospective distinction: Proposed prompt/workflow modifications concern future agent capability rather than a response to a present task. - Adaptation option generated: A structured candidate with hypothesis, expectedbenefit and allowed artifact changes, or nochange. - Path back into current capability / S3: The accepted content-addressed version is loaded/verified for subsequent task runs, with rollback after unsuccessful deployment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses the substantive candidate hypothesis and changes; deterministic gates can reject it but do not invent the adaptation option. - Evidence: proposal reasoning, engine proposer wiring, improvement, deployment. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Autonomous adaptation requires configured reasoning backend and provider; a candidate is not deployed without human-selected test thresholds and successful tests. Source evidence is not a live improvement-outcome claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party identity/ultimate-policy dispute resolution and binding return for the RSI installation is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: What targets, prompts, models, budgets and artifact fields should be legitimately allowed to change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human operator fixes target schema, allowed keys, model configuration and evaluation rules at initialization; no identity-policy issue is referred to an adjudicator for resolution. - Decision owner: Operator/developer outside the live run; model proposer is restricted to editable lower-level artifact fields. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable config hash, key allowlist, model identity snapshots, validation errors and manual stop. - Closure path: Fixed policy blocks out-of-bounds proposals; it does not carry an identity question to a legitimate policy owner and feed a fresh ruling back into operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Artifact edits and accepted versions adjust capability, not ultimate purpose/constitutional identity; immutable settings are enforcement not adjudication. - Evidence: definition, improvement, engine. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A developer may deliberately install new policies in a new workspace, but that is not a first-party governed S5 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/35a76015978b6753215beac6e12cb62e542ba822/assessments/rs/rsi-flow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 35a76015978b6753215beac6e12cb62e542ba822\">35a7601 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T19:32:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kumizeod/AgentForeman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentForeman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kumizeod/AgentForeman/tree/634f9c0906f133fc577d6291c29b8f8cfd6af02a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 634f9c0906f133fc577d6291c29b8f8cfd6af02a\">634f9c0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce candidate code/experiments and general task artifacts through model decisions with returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous goals, failed code/training, poor candidates and contradictory evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose experiment/repair/retest/stop and instructions; author source or artifacts; revise after independent criticism. - Decision owner: configured model-backed manager, coding worker, contributors and integrator, invoked by first-party protocols. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source snapshots, typed responses, worker pool leases, edit allowlist, native training/checks, archives and round/deadline bounds. - Closure path: verified baseline → manager model choice → worker action → independent measurement/criticism → next choice or revision → preserved terminal result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model roles leaves deterministic transport and checks but no substantive hypothesis, edit or report decision. - Caveats: A is structural ownership, not proof of live model quality; external coding tools are credited only as explicitly assembled task actors. - Boundary reachability: CLI campaign/overnight and cooperate/debate commands assemble these roles and execute their chosen actions; they are shipped supported modes, not test-only prompts. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/manager.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/engine.py; src/agentforeman/cooperate.py; src/agentforeman/debate.py; src/agentforeman/poolworker.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no separately closed inter-S1 interference regulation at the declared task boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent contributions can be inconsistent; worker contention and duplicate execution are mechanically bounded. - Decisive decision or feedback right: integrator chooses combined output, but contributors are not subsequently coordinated through a specific inter-unit correction loop. - Decision owner: integrator owns S1 synthesis; runtime owns fixed sequencing/leases, with no separately evidenced autonomous S2 discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: independent assignments, integration contract, queue/lease locks, quota cooldown and critique of combined artifacts. - Closure path: contributors return once → integrator revises the combined product; duplicate jobs are suppressed by fixed IDs/locks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: decomposition, waiting for all contributions and rewriting one combined answer do not establish mutual adjustment of operational units. - Caveats: does not deny coordination in a broader organization assembled around this tool. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/cooperate.py; src/agentforeman/pool.py; docs/COOPERATIVE.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system discretionary current-control function over a portfolio established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate failure, incumbent comparison, quota waits and bounded task duration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: manager chooses the next experiment within one fixed objective; it cannot change resources, task goal or budgets. - Decision owner: model owns local S1 research choices; operator/configuration selects task scope, role pool and limits; runtime enforces those choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: campaign rounds/best record, budget/deadline checks, role registry, UI status, cancel and resume. - Closure path: measured rounds return to a next-task-action choice; generic stop/confirmation returns no evidenced whole-system resource bargain. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the campaign manager name does not turn its local experimental policy into a metasystem; fixed quotas are enforcement. - Caveats: broader multi-project allocation may be performed externally but is outside the declared first-party campaign mode. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/manager.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/briefing.py; src/agentforeman/pool.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge operational claims through separate model audit/critique with complementary original evidence and corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported synthesis claims, missed criticism, inadequate experiment plans and false candidate success. - Decisive decision or feedback right: distinct critic/auditor chooses anchored findings and accept/revise/reject; campaign reviewer can block or return an executable proposal for revision. - Decision owner: separately invoked model reviewer/auditor roles; deterministic gates apply verdict consistency and run trusted checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct role identities, read-only review snapshot, source/check/artifact anchors, issue ledger, frozen verifier and bounded revision/retest. - Closure path: raw artifacts/source/check receipts → separate critic judgment → integrator/manager revision and recheck, or stopped/needs-evidence/rejected result; council audit conditions final disposition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model audit judgment owns substantive criticism; deterministic parsing and trusted checkpoint measurement are support, not the owner of that judgment. - Caveats: distinct identities are not statistically independent models; supplied sources are not authenticated by quote matching, and live factual correctness remains unmeasured. - Boundary reachability: documented campaign --review, cooperative distinct-reviewer mode and council audit are shipped runtime modes; no developer fixture is needed to select them. - Claim being audited: candidate/proposal or chair/integrator output is supported by original source material, controls, actual artifacts and check results. - Ordinary reporting path: manager rationale, proposer artifacts and chair/integrator summary. - Complementary access path: reviewer receives copied source snapshots plus experiment evidence; general critics receive full artifact/source/check anchor documents, and council auditor sees original answers and all criticism rather than chair summary alone. - Independence boundary: separate read-only sessions/role identities from author/integrator/chair; no authorship permission for the reviewer. Trusted verifier directly reads tensors without importing candidate code, adding corroboration rather than autonomous judgment. - Who acts on findings: campaign manager/integrator revises under recorded issue IDs; controller blocks/returns or marks needsevidence/rejected instead of fabricating success. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/discussion.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/debate.py; src/agentforeman/council.py; src/agentforeman/templates/moons/verify.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective adaptation loop changing AgentForeman operational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new research hypotheses, failed experiment results, provider quota changes and user task uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: manager may change candidate experiment code, not harness capability/organization; task chair clarifies current requirements. - Decision owner: task agents own research outcomes; maintainer/operator owns external product evolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: research plans, measurements, council sources, quota information and roadmap. - Closure path: research findings return to the current task; no prospective environmental judgment returns as a change to harness capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research as the primary operational transformation is S1; internal optimization and quota reaction do not establish S4. - Caveats: an operator could use outputs to redesign its organization, but that external act is not a shipped adaptation closure. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/manager.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/council.py; src/agentforeman/briefing.py; docs/ROADMAP.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop established beyond bounded task contracts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unclear scope, changed deliverables/acceptance/autonomy and stale user confirmation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human confirms a task contract; agents must pause when they report a scope change, rather than decide ultimate policy. - Decision owner: operator owns the local task mandate; product identity remains maintainer-defined outside runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: version/fingerprint confirmation, allowed-decision/pause fields, frozen checks, scope-change signal and stop controls. - Closure path: briefing → exact human confirmation → bound child task; requested scope changes pause for new clarification. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: this is operational task admission and authority bounding; it is not an identity/ultimate-policy dispute between present operations and future adaptation. - Caveats: contract editing is a real returned human decision but does not by itself satisfy the function-first S5 threshold. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/briefing.py; src/agentforeman/taskcontract.py; src/agentforeman/debate.py; docs/BRIEFING.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8100f851f4f20a52d8b11424bbccf95b1d296801/assessments/ag/agentforeman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8100f851f4f20a52d8b11424bbccf95b1d296801\">8100f85 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T20:13:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/3ae3ae/harness2-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness² CLI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/3ae3ae/harness2-cli/tree/0c811344a4edfe34960b2fc8f286369d7e81fb27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0c811344a4edfe34960b2fc8f286369d7e81fb27\">0c81134 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Bounded model-backed improvement work per encountered task: examine operational evidence, edit a harness, probe the edited candidate and produce a selected task harness plus result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different user problems and actual coding-agent failures demand different instruction, skill, script or hook changes; candidate edits can also violate a harness component contract. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured improver chooses candidate file changes and explains them against evidence. The model selector (in automatic mode) chooses a final task result where multiple candidates exist. - Decision owner: Model-backed proposer/editor and optional automatic selector, invoked through the first-party session interface. External executor cognition is a dependency, but the improvement goal and agent-edit control path are shipped by the reviewed CLI. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace harvest, component allowlist, tree validation, per-stage retry/fallback, rollout adapter, result/trajectory records and candidate selection parser. - Closure path: Current base-task rollout → independent agent-edited harness trees → validated candidate task probes → composed candidate and selector result → accepted task-harness and evidence for later reuse. - Boundary reachability: cli.run constructs Proposer with the installed channel config and invokes schedule.runsweep; runtask calls recursion.runmode, whose runstage actually invokes Proposer.call with writable context/harness and captures its changed tree, then probes candidates. All are included in the shipped CLI mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model makes contentful harness edits and, when configured, evidence-driven task-result choices; source-local Python merely bounds and enforces those decisions. Dry-run, static tests and deterministic fixtures by themselves do not prove model autonomy. - Evidence: run.py, recursion.py, proposer.py, schedule.py. - Basis: Supported live-model session path at the frozen commit, not asserted empirical success rate. - Confidence: High for source-wired decision ownership; outcome quality untested. - Caveats: The target coding assistant and provider are externally configured and not extra first-party viable S1 units.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No evidenced S2-specific stabilization among two distinct, separately viable owned S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent candidate and domain execution might consume resources, but distinct S1 divisions and an inter-unit oscillation/collision control relation are not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic concurrency width, domain ordering, semaphore and run-label isolation coordinate experimental steps, not an agentic inter-S1 conflict response. - Decision owner: Static Python scheduler and operator configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ThreadPoolExecutor, proposer semaphore, namespace isolation, mode/domain libraries and run budget. - Closure path: Scheduled experiments complete and aggregate; no S2-specific sensed interference and returned reciprocal adjustment across autonomous operating units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Stage-level parallelism, composition and result pooling are parts of one bounded optimization process, not S2 because they run concurrently. - Evidence: schedule.py, recursion.py, proposer.py. - Basis: Scoped source negative after examining task and domain concurrency. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An external multi-agent product using this CLI might provide S2 independently.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-current control relationship over independently viable operational divisions at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Budget overruns, candidate failures, task completion, user approval and project file drift are local run/deployment constraints rather than multi-unit resource/commitment governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The operator sets execution budget, task set and allowed surface; runtime enforces limits; selectors compare results. None revises current commitments across distinct S1 units with systemic authority. - Decision owner: CLI user for configuration, deterministic scheduler for enforcement and model for candidate choice; no separately supported S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Budget/call caps, run status, concurrency limit, retries, apply conflict checks and --force option. - Closure path: Fixed run settings → bounded task experimentation → output; absent whole-current S3 view, discretionary priority/resource intervention and returned binding adjustment to multiple ongoing units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Picking a better harness is an operational or prospective adaptation choice; enforcing an operator&#39;s preset budget is not agent-owned S3 and ordinary apply approval is not parent-governed whole-system control. - Evidence: run.py, schedule.py, apply.py. - Basis: Bounded negative of whole-organization current-control closure. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Operator may manage a larger organization, but its current control is not implemented by this application.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently complementary audit channel challenges routine S1–S3 reports and closes findings into corrective systemic control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Bad candidate harness files, inaccurate agent result claims and poor task answers are checked by local tree validation, rollout comparisons, benchmark judging or optional evaluation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Ordinary candidate validators, task selector and eval compare outputs; none has separately independent authority to audit a standing S3 report and route corrective findings to management. - Decision owner: Deterministic validators/evaluation routines, configured model selector or human comparator within the same adaptation process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Harness component checks, anonymous candidate selection, result reports and user-triggered eval. - Closure path: Candidate/result evidence → within-loop selection and optional later quality measurement; no complementary audit → binding whole-current corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent improver proposals and blind selection reduce some bias, but are routine production/evaluation, not an independent S3 oversight path. - Evidence: selection.py, eval.py, judge.py. - Basis: Scoped negative distinguishing quality checks from complementary audit independence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A separate external QA team could supply such audit for a deployed agent but is outside this reviewed system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: Model-backed across-task prospective harness adaptation with a second, explicitly supported parent-controlled blind-selection mode that changes which candidate and lesson enter the future task library. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar project tasks, newly observed errors and weak transfer of task-specific improvements to later tasks; user may also disagree with automated candidate choice. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Automatic selector chooses the adopted candidate and reflector extracts a transferable lesson; in --selector human mode the user personally selects among blinded final candidates while a model reflector can distill the adopted result. The choice feeds subsequent tasks through the library. - Decision owner: A: model-backed selector and reflector/proposer in CLI sweep; P: operator at the shipped interactive selector, not the benchmark author or external research institute. Each mode has separately attributable adoption authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-domain sequential Library, selectionpool, anonymous candidate files, human TTY prompt/choice, adopted tree and experience persistence, context.build injection into later proposer contexts; final project deployment is optional with apply. - Closure path: Encounters and candidate execution evidence → automatic/human chosen task harness → model-reflected generalizable lesson → modelib.add → next task&#39;s proposal context includes earlier domain library → potential changed subsequent harness. After a run, generated consolidated harness may also be explicitly applied to project files. - Boundary reachability: Shipped cli/run.py chooses HumanSelector when selector.channel.kind == human; otherwise it instantiates model-backed Proposer. Both invoke schedule.runsweep and selection.runselection; schedule.runonemode calls reflectadoption and conditionally modelib.add, while next task&#39;s runstage loads the library. cli/apply.py separately permits reviewed real project-file adoption. - External distinction: Actual newly encountered user task domains and contrasted coding-agent trajectories expose operational shortcomings not specified in a fixed harness. - Future / prospective distinction: Per-domain transferable lessons plus consolidated project harness target future tasks, rather than merely grading or repairing one immediate deliverable. - Adaptation option generated: Model editor proposes changed skills/instructions/scripts/hooks; model/human selector adopts a tested candidate; reflector writes future-reuse cues; consolidation proposes project-wide next-task harness. - Path back into current capability / S3: Selected experience is persisted in the per-domain library and read by subsequent model proposer sessions, changing the options considered for later edits. The optional apply command installs consolidated or one-task adopted components into the user&#39;s project; no independent S3 manager is asserted. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A has agent-owned candidate choice and lesson generation with model decisions returning through first-party memory to later operations. The distinct P mode assigns the same consequential selection right to the user through a real terminal interaction and returns it through the same library. This does not assign the separately guarded final installation to the model. - Evidence: run.py, human.py, selection.py, schedule.py, library.py, apply.py. - Basis: The two first-party shipped prospective adaptation loops share the persistent future-facing library, but have distinct actual adoption decision owners. - Confidence: High for reachable branch/closure, medium for model generalization since no model runs were made. - Caveats: --selector human requires a TTY and multiple finalists (k &gt; 1); at k=1 selection is trivial. A failed reflection yields no transferable lesson. Project apply defaults to human approval and is not evidence of autonomous project deployment.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate ultimate identity/mission-policy adjudication is shipped for the overall improvement organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Operator task selection, allowed editable files, model/provider choice, benchmark scope and safety limits bound execution but do not create a separate ultimate policy decision loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User chooses and configures the run and can approve/force project-file writes; no identity-level conflict is escalated to a legitimate authority and returned as governing policy. - Decision owner: External developer/project owner for the overall purpose; shipped runtime for validation and configured model for bounded edits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI config and setup, component tree validator, source boundaries, apply confirmation and git-dirty checks. - Closure path: User-provided goal and constraints → local optimization and optional installation; absent system identity dispute → authority judgment → returned revised governing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Supervised application is an operational/capability adoption gate, not by itself an identity- or ethos-level S5 decision. The parent-mode S4 classification does not automatically imply S5=P. - Evidence: settings.py, run.py, apply.py. - Basis: Bounded negative for identity-level authority and operational return. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The user&#39;s broader software organization may have S5; this repository does not establish its governance authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b4570aa846bcb5c7e3c02ccabec88b26877d4335/assessments/ha/harness-cli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b4570aa846bcb5c7e3c02ccabec88b26877d4335\">b4570aa &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T18:56:20+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amosblomqvist/learnharness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LearnHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amosblomqvist/learnharness/tree/3dc88472eea41eed0d5e5f29c2f16f8d063c415b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3dc88472eea41eed0d5e5f29c2f16f8d063c415b\">3dc8847 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform goal-directed teaching, research, demonstration construction and visual/PDF assistance under a chosen lesson mission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain starting competence, unexpected wrong answers, unfamiliar facts, tool failures and domain-specific problems requiring outside evidence or visualization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Active prober/teacher models choose questions, explanation revisions, quiz checks, graph lookup and specialist delegation; specialist models choose searches, fetches, demo edits/tests and visual construction steps. - Decision owner: Shipped real Agent and runSubagent loops invoked through first-party models and tools; mere persistence/UI are not S1 decision owners. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-turn persisted reconstruction, probe-to-teach seam, schema-validated question/plan tools, model selection and BYOK gates, structured worker toolsets, source-specific job persistence and abort handling. - Closure path: Learner goal/response → prober or teacher model decides action → asks/teaches/checks, or dispatches a specialist model → learner answers or worker returns evidence/artifact → teacher uses new information in following action. - Boundary reachability: handleChatRequest constructs buildAgent for the prober/teacher and jobTools dispatches to real shipped subagent runners; explicit configured model keys are needed. Scripted test engines do not supply the credited autonomy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model agents select semantically different learning questions, explanation paths, search/fix iterations and specialist output; fixed question grading and JSON work queues alone cannot choose these. - Evidence: teacher runtime, job tool dispatch, researcher with web tools, demo builder with real headless check. - Basis: Shipped, model-backed adaptive operation and separately executable agent specialties. - Confidence: High source-path, medium live-provider efficacy (not executed). - Caveats: BYOK and required tool capabilities are deployment prerequisites. Tests use fake engines and do not validate pedagogical outcomes with real users.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Would attenuate a conflict/oscillation specifically between independent lesson S1 workers and feed a revised coordination commitment into those workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing background job completions, shared graph writes, multiple specialist reports or two S1 units reaching incompatible conclusions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Per-chat wake/steer deduplication and global graph audit lock serialize events; the teacher commissions specialists and receives their reports. No first-party autonomous inter-specialist disagreement negotiation or adjusted joint commitments is shown. - Decision owner: Deterministic job registry and semaphore control the mechanics; teacher handles the educational consequence as its S3/current-lesson decision, not a distinct inter-S1 S2 arbitration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-chat serialized wake delivery and recovery, durable pending result IDs, global limited concurrency, source-local extraction single-flight and global serialized audit/merge. - Closure path: Queued worker completion is delivered to teacher once; two graph audits cannot concurrently read and write a stale graph. This is mechanical admission/stability without a distinctive semantic response returned to the conflicting S1 workers. - Boundary reachability: The named locks, duplicate IDs, consent gates and worker result steers are live first-party source paths, but no separate model mediator resolves S1-vs-S1 work collision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Correct sequencing protects state, yet methodology requires specific inter-S1 disturbance, distinct discretionary response and returned adjustment of S1 behaviour, not delegation or a queue alone. - Evidence: serialized delivery / wake protocol, graph audit gate and source single-flight, six-system topology. - Basis: Bounded negative for agent-owned inter-S1 conflict attenuation; credible structural serialization is not enough. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A user can ask the teacher to reconcile contradictory reports; no dedicated shipped inter-S1 coordination mandate is thereby established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Keep one current learning session coherent across the learner&#39;s goal, assessed understanding, dependency plan, teaching steps and commissioned specialist work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Learner confusion invalidating current plan assumptions, wrong facts, delayed external support, mismatch between learning target and work in progress, specialist failures and changed user constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The teacher has the current session transcript and goal, authors/revises the visible plan, decides which knowledge gap matters now, allocates research/demo/visual/PDF work and changes explanation/check sequence based on returned worker results and learner answers. - Decision owner: Model-bearing first-party teacher for the session-local teaching mission; learner can change the overall request, but deterministic planinit/queue enforcement is not the author of a new plan or delegation decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Prober handoff, current session record, DAG validator, question node tags, job input and result steers, durable wake, one-turn active registry, disabled-tool and model-role settings. - Closure path: Prober establishes starting point → teacher builds global lesson dependency tree → learner&#39;s answers or independent job results reveal a current gap → teacher revises explanation/plan and initiates/cancels/uses supporting work → next quiz/lesson state reflects that decision. - Boundary reachability: buildAgent(&quot;teacher&quot;) is attached after the real probedone handoff, has plan and job tool access in the supported chat pipeline; background worker result messages return to this same persistent session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: It is more than a blind router: the teacher decides a session-wide pedagogical priority and changes ongoing commitment to the goal in response to actual feedback. It does not manage the entire multi-session application, unrelated practice sets or a global provider budget. - Whole-system current view: Session transcript, learner goal, active plan/progress, commissioned worker result steers, and optional read-only graph inspection relevant to the session. - Current-control decision scope: Revise dependency plan/teaching sequence, task requests to multiple agents and response to produced evidence within the declared one-session recursion. - Evidence: teacher planning and on-the-fly repair, tool registry and model loop, worker feedback into live teacher, job instruction and correction. - Basis: Source-closed current session-wide semantic decision/return using autonomous teacher. Not a claim of product-wide S3 at higher recursion. - Confidence: Medium; stronger for declared one-session recursion than a broader multi-session organization. - Caveats: Plan progress tags are deterministic, not a second autonomous controller. No source proof of reliable cognitive diagnosis is inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Reconcile the identity/novelty claim of graph propositions extracted from the session against separately read existing graph evidence before committing the update. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Duplicate concepts minted by distinct extraction runs or different sessions, same fact paraphrased as different graph nodes, or identity links based on stale graph snapshots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A distinct model auditor explores the existing graph through find/listsessions/listpropositions and submits links; mergeFindings uses those judgments under the audit lock. It cannot inspect or fact-check the raw teaching source. - Decision owner: Separate first-party auditor agent makes semantic identity judgments, with deterministic serialization and merge applying them. No independent raw-source verification or challenge to extractor factual truth is present, so classify a narrow complementary constructor C, not general-purpose closed independent S3 A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Schema-bounded auditor submit, graph exploration tools, process-wide FIFO audit/merge lock, deterministic ledger writes, coverage checkpoints, rollback-on-stage-failure and source provenance. - Closure path: Extractor proposes lesson propositions → isolated auditor sees proposed propositions plus independently queried existing graph → returns identity links → merger collapses duplicate concepts or adds new ones → teacher can subsequently query the updated graph; no verified correction of teaching claims is claimed. - Boundary reachability: Enabled graph extraction invokes runAuditor in the standard source-to-ledger pipeline and mergeFindings consumes its output; BYOK extractor/auditor keys are required. It is a mandatory same-path graph integrity check, not a standing out-of-band lesson review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The auditor selects identity links through model exploration rather than code-only deduplication, but its complementary scope is narrow and the raw-source evidence boundary excludes stronger autonomous verification claims. - Claim being audited: Extractor&#39;s implicit assertion that newly proposed graph propositions are unique rather than semantic duplicates of an existing concept. - Ordinary reporting path: Extractor&#39;s submitted proposition list and source-local digest, subsequently represented as new graph nodes. - Complementary access path: Separate graph-aware model agent gets the live existing graph through first-party find/list tools while the extractor is isolated from that graph&#39;s full cross-source state. - Independence boundary: Distinct agent role, prompt and tools; no access to original source text, same application and possible underlying provider model; must not be confused with epistemic truth-checking. - Who acts on findings: The deterministic merger applies links immediately; the resulting graph is read-only exposed to future teacher sessions and the learner. - Evidence: auditor mandate and source exclusion, independent graph exploration tools, graph merge/evidence ledger. - Basis: Active model-backed narrow identity-audit constructor with complementary graph access; no independently validated correctness of the input claims. - Confidence: Medium for S3 constructor, high for its explicit limitations. - Caveats: Headless demo-browser tests occur inside the producing S1 worker and are not independent S3. A routine verifier in the same ingest path should not be promoted to a comprehensive audit of the lesson; future independent same-ref review could reasonably bound this to — if graph identity verification is considered inseparable production validation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Would scout changing external/future learning or agent capabilities and return options that alter the lesson organization&#39;s capability rather than just answer a current fact question. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future model/tool shifts, curriculum/environment changes and novel teaching methods outside the present learner request. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Researcher retrieves outside evidence only for teacher&#39;s current informational need; learner/operator selects provider, prompt override and enabled tools by settings. No standing future-oriented strategy process is shown. - Decision owner: Teacher for current accuracy and human for manually changed app settings, not a distinct autonomous S4 opportunity/threat agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Search/fetch researcher, durable cross-session knowledge graph, provider roles, prompt/settings UI and saved learner records. - Closure path: External research returns a cited brief into the current lesson and can correct facts, while persistent graph can inform later sessions; neither source path generates and adopts a prospective capability-change strategy. - Boundary reachability: Actual researcher and settings/graph surfaces exist; no future-option-to-S3 contract is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fetching recent facts and cumulative memory do not themselves create an outside-and-then intelligence function. - Evidence: researcher prompt and web tools, teacher research requirement, global model settings. - Basis: Bounded negative: external information serves the current session instead of strategic adaptation. - Confidence: High for reviewed standard path. - Caveats: A user may ask about future trends as a lesson topic; the topic alone does not make its executor S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Would resolve an ultimate identity/purpose legitimacy conflict for the learning organization with binding authority and return a governing policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting fundamental educational goals or competing legitimacy claims rather than everyday disagreement about the next lesson step. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Learner chooses or changes the study goal and app configuration; teacher&#39;s instructions prescribe a method and ask the learner to approve the plan. No standing binding constitutional-purpose adjudication is wired. - Decision owner: Human learner sets the mission external to the autonomous lesson, teacher regulates subordinate pedagogical commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Probe/goal question, approval before teaching, role prompts, per-user provider and prompt settings. - Closure path: Learner can change the study target, and the next session turn adapts. This is ordinary task definition and plan review, not an adjudicated fundamental identity-policy dispute. - Boundary reachability: Goal elicitation and plan confirmation are active, but identity-level disagreement resolution and binding organizational policy are not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A mandatory teaching philosophy in a system prompt is static implementation policy, not runtime S5 authority. - Evidence: probe/teach split, teacher&#39;s plan confirmation, fixed teaching identity. - Basis: Bounded negative for first-party policy legitimacy closure. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A learner remains the legitimate authority to select its task, but task selection does not on its own justify S5=P at the present recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e0377deccfa4315a6ad15781c29c8fa36bea6903/assessments/le/learnharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e0377deccfa4315a6ad15781c29c8fa36bea6903\">e0377de &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:18:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Moissanite-Labs/exo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">exo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Moissanite-Labs/exo/tree/e9ad895d2c59e998f0358b982a9b8a4ac0ad2aea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e9ad895d2c59e998f0358b982a9b8a4ac0ad2aea\">e9ad895 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: persistent coding/assistant work with discretionary tool use and delegated subtasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying tasks, repository state, tool/provider errors and interrupted sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose tools, edits, commands and follow-up steps from current observations. - Decision owner: the main or configured delegated agent in the first-party Loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CoreEngine composition, router, tool runner, plugins/approval desk, context tree and typed result persistence. - Closure path: tool results are appended to messages before the next model round; steering and errors update subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: compose.create builds the current core, registers the delegator, connects runtool and starts Loop; no legacy Hermes host is required for this claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model discretion leaves tool/transport/persistence mechanisms without task-level transformation decisions. - Evidence: src/exo/compose.py; src/exo/core/engine.py; src/exo/core/loop.py; src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider APIs/claude-cli can supply inference; their unrelated harness functions are not borrowed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no bounded first-party inter-S1 coordination function established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generic child concurrency and shared-log consistency do not establish a concrete inter-S1 interference/attenuation/feedback witness. The writer lock excludes competing cores of the same persistent agent, not mutually adjusted S1 work cells; peer messaging/delegation alone is insufficient. - Evidence: src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py; src/exo/core/agentlog.py; src/exo/peers.py; src/exo/core/engine.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-current organizational control decision loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task decomposition, narrow task intervention and constructor-selected limits do not independently establish whole-system resource/commitment regulation. The reviewed commands expose current state but no additional function-specific S3 decision/return path beyond ordinary operations was evidenced; no P inferred from a generic operator UI. - Evidence: src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py; src/exo/subagents.py; src/exo/core/engine.py; src/exo/modelspend/policy.py; src/exo/scheduler.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit loop established for current operational claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Trace and findings are first-party observability/self-report with deterministic rechecks; generic critic delegation lacks an audit-specific claim/access/corrective contract. Learning critique and held-out adaptation testing concern prospective proposals, not a separately closed operational audit. No S3 is inferred from audit/reviewer terminology. - Evidence: docs/TRACE.md; src/exo/core/engine.py; src/exo/core/findings.py; src/exo/core/tools/builtin/harness.py; src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py; src/exo/learn/review.py; src/exo/learn/climb.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: adapt future behavior to the external principal&#39;s recurring needs and corrected preferences through typed, evidence-linked options. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current behavior no longer matches principal corrections/rulings or generalizable class-of-task expectations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: derive a beyond-instance lesson and adopt an eligible future behavior rule; alternatively the principal explicitly accepts/rejects a proposed adaptation. - Decision owner: base mode: aux reviewer/critic supplies adaptive judgment and bounded automatic promotion closes eligible ordinary lessons. Parent mode: legitimate store operator/principal decides the concrete proposal. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: principal authorship filtering, evidence/session/weight constraints, guarded/conflict holds, proposal diffs, promotion ledger and refreshed memory nodes. - Closure path: eligible autonomous promotions or explicit proposalaccept write accepted nodes/skills and ledger state; exo-memory reads nodes fresh for subsequent turns. Guarded/skill paths do not silently bypass their gates. - Boundary reachability: compose attaches learn to the current engine; onturnend starts review for new principal signals; review calls constrained applypolicy. CLI exo proposals exposes show/accept/reject, and accepted nodes return through installed memory. Optional slow API/fixture export is not the basis of automatic A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model develops the prospective behavioral lesson from external principal feedback; deterministic policy constrains its acceptance. In the explicit review mode the principal owns adoption. Mere transcript retention, fact recall or a roadmap is not the credited S4 witness. - Evidence: src/exo/compose.py; src/exo/learn/learner.py; src/exo/learn/review.py; src/exo/learn/promote.py; src/exo/consolidate.py; docs/LEARNING.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: base claim is limited to enabled store-backed, eligible generalizable behavior lessons; facts stored without a prospective behavior implication are not S4. Skill/held-out nightly automatic scheduling is not claimed; no empirical effectiveness or transfer result.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: retain ultimate authority over the harness&#39;s public/irreversible/financial risk-policy boundaries at the principal recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runtime gate policy changes could relax reserved authority or alter ultimate allowed exposure/risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adopt or withhold a changed governing gate policy, rather than merely approve one action. - Decision owner: the authenticated live principal; agent/peer input cannot supply that credential. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: principal transport/authorship checks, pinned policy snapshot, changed-policy hashes, stricter interim enforcement and signed acceptance record. - Closure path: gate status exposes changed policy; /gate accept requires principal authority, records policyaccept and replaces the pinned policy used to govern later calls. - Boundary reachability: installed exo-gate command routes accept to acceptpolicies; the current composed core loads plugins and carries principal authority through the transport path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ultimate policy adoption remains with the principal; deterministic classification, hashes and individual exact-text approval are supporting controls, not autonomous S5. - Evidence: plugins/exo-gate/exogate/hook.py; src/exo/principal.py; src/exo/compose.py; docs/GATE.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: bounded to the legitimate principal&#39;s ultimate risk/delegation policy; no claim that every approval, SOUL.md edit or maintainer action is S5. Required-executor behavior is mode-specific. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: who may authorize public/irreversible/financial acts and whether changed reserved-risk policy becomes governing. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: principal transport authority checked by requireprincipal; no internal autonomous S5 mode established. - Return-to-operation path: changed policy status/hash → live principal accept → logged policyaccept → new pinned policy → subsequent tool decisions.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37735444207e5ea17cd166d25eaa1dde9259c22e/assessments/mo/moissanite-labs-exo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37735444207e5ea17cd166d25eaa1dde9259c22e\">3773544 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
